^ Cite error: The named reference Prime was invoked but never defined (see the help page).', 'Ended', 'Nickelodeon', 'Water. Earth. Fire. Air.', 8.755, 4748, '2005-02-21', '2008-07-19', 28.5488, 'en', 'Avatar: The Last Airbender', 'https://www.nick.com/shows/avatar-the-last-airbender', 61, 3, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/9RQhVb3r3mCMqYVhLoCu4EvuipP.jpg', '/kU98MbVVgi72wzceyrEbClZmMFe.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:40:47Z', '2026-04-03T17:40:53Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('567aa148-b059-4359-84f6-a3c38cf2b0ae', 'Bernard''s Watch', 15697, 71925, 1997, 'British children''s drama series about a young boy who could stop time with a magical pocket watch.', 'The first run told of a young boy called Bernard, played by David Peachey, from Oakwood, Nottinghamshire, who was always late, until a postman gave him a "magic watch" which could stop time. He soon found out that the postman had magical powers, and that these watches were given to people who needed them. The rules of him keeping his watch were that he must not use it to commit crimes or hurt anyone. Every episode focused on Bernard or someone to whom he''d lent the watch facing a problem or simply doing day-to-day things and trying to sort them out, using the watch.

The second run was significantly different to the original. According to the final opening sequence, the watch simply flew through Bernard''s window. The character of the "postman" did not exist, and Bernard seemed to be the only person in the world who had such a watch. His best friend Nathan knew about the watch. This series revolved mainly around Bernard''s school, Pentup Primary, where he''d usually get the better of his bullying school teacher Ms Yvonne Savage and a popular girl called Nicolette who is his arch rival.

As well as the TV series, Andrew Norriss also wrote a novelisation based on the first three series of the show in 1999. In the context of the novel, Bernard received the watch from an elderly aunt, who had received it from her husband who acquired it from an old friend, although they never knew how the friend had obtained the watch as by the time he passed it on he had suffered a stroke and couldn''t speak. Unlike in the TV show where using the watch was harmless, the watch has a subtle side-effect on the user in that it uses up the iron molecules in their blood, leaving regular users vulnerable to anaemia requiring them to consume iron supplements on a regular basis, causing Bernard to collapse when he avoids taking the iron-tainted drink provided by his aunt because he didn''t like the taste and was unaware of the side-effects of the watch. On a personal note, Bernard is only living with his father, as his mother died before the events of the novel, and Karen is also shown as living with a single parent with her mother having divorced before she was born. At the novel''s conclusion, the watch is briefly stolen by a woman who once worked as a housekeeper for Bernard''s aunt, but she is captured when the watch runs down and she tries to threaten Bernard for the key. Ill at various points throughout the book, Bernard''s aunt dies in the penultimate chapter, but her spirit appears to him after her death to tell him that the true purpose of the watch is for ''learning''. In the final chapter, Bernard uses the watch key on a chain his aunt gave him to wind it back up, Bernard and Karen enjoying the restoration of the watch as Bernard concludes that he has time to learn what she meant by that comment.', 'Ended', 'CITV', '', 6.3, 6, '1997-11-14', '2005-03-31', 1.9246, 'en', 'Bernard''s Watch', '', 79, 7, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/5bBB7Q3ekDGAqPzprfmpB11aGj0.jpg', '/s0n7MNnBXiskWkPVPhtF3r2FcGK.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:20:03Z', '2026-04-03T17:20:11Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('570bf5c2-32b3-408a-8336-4c07e7b9a3da', 'The Real Housewives of Miami', 34268, 228691, 2011, 'Six of the most influential and connected women live life to the fullest in the sunny city where both the party and the drama never stop.', '*The Real Housewives of Miami* premiered on February 22, 2011, originally conceived as a dinner-party-centric series titled *Miami Social Club* before being absorbed into the larger *Real Housewives* franchise. The first season introduced an ensemble consisting of Lea Black, Adriana de Moura, Alexia Echevarria, Marysol Patton, Larsa Pippen, and Cristy Rice. Early storylines highlighted the women''s lavish lifestyles, Pippen''s demanding parenting style, and Patton''s eccentric mother, Elsa, who quickly became a fan-favorite figure. Following a poorly received first season, the series underwent a significant revamp. Pippen and Rice departed, and the second season introduced Lisa Hochstein, Joanna Krupa, Karent Sierra, and Ana Quincoces. The revamped second season delivered heightened drama, heavily centering on an infamous physical altercation between Krupa and de Moura at a lingerie party. Other storylines included Hochstein''s fertility struggles, Sierra''s inability to connect with the group, and Echevarria temporarily stepping down to a recurring capacity to care for her son Frankie following a near-fatal car accident.

The third season saw the departure of Sierra and the return of Echevarria to the main cast. The season''s narrative was dominated by the spectacular collapse of Black and de Moura''s long-standing friendship after it was revealed that de Moura had been secretly married to her fiancé for several years, leading Black to feel financially and emotionally exploited. The season ultimately culminated in the extravagant, dueling weddings of Krupa and de Moura, alongside Patton''s emotional struggles with her mother''s declining health. Despite a dramatic third season, a decline in ratings led Bravo to place the series on an indefinite hiatus, leaving the show effectively canceled for nearly a decade.

In 2021, the series was officially revived for a fourth season on the streaming platform Peacock. Echevarria, Hochstein, and Pippen returned as main cast members, joined by event planner Guerdy Abraira, anesthesiologist Dr. Nicole Martin, and former Russian beauty queen Julia Lemigova, who made franchise history as the first LGBTQ+ housewife through her marriage to tennis legend Martina Navratilova. Meanwhile, de Moura and Patton returned in "friend of" capacities alongside newcomer Kiki Barth. The revival season followed Pippen re-establishing her life in Miami following her high-profile divorce from Scottie Pippen, and Echevarria''s emotional journey to marry her fiancé Todd Nepola amidst family conflicts and the sudden passing of her mother.

The series continued to build momentum in its fifth season, which captured the devastating, highly publicized, and deeply acrimonious collapse of Hochstein''s marriage to plastic surgeon Lenny Hochstein after he unexpectedly left her for a younger woman. Simultaneously, Pippen sparked controversy with her new relationship with Marcus Jordan, the son of her ex-husband''s former teammate, and ignited a fierce feud with Martin after spreading rumors about the doctor''s professional conduct. The show transitioned back to Bravo for its sixth season, which profoundly focused on Abraira''s shocking breast cancer diagnosis. Abraira''s health battle caused significant friction with Pippen, whom she accused of maliciously leaking the diagnosis to the rest of the cast. Elsewhere, Lemigova and Navratilova navigated the prospect of adoption following Navratilova''s own dual cancer battles, while Echevarria faced mounting scrutiny from the women over her and Nepola''s abrupt change in living situations. Following the success of the revival seasons, the series was subsequently renewed for a seventh season.', 'Returning Series', 'Bravo', 'Same heat. New shade.', 5.5, 21, '2011-02-22', '2025-10-16', 3.9908, 'en', 'The Real Housewives of Miami', 'https://www.bravotv.com/the-real-housewives-of-miami', 114, 7, 1, 'Reality', 'US', '/mlhCJsrZ2QsZmMuvI66RUhPAy3h.jpg', '/z1Wj0JhRF2AN6zSYsk5moptCfVA.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:35:09Z', '2026-04-03T16:54:47Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('5763807e-668f-42f4-9f90-716799017463', 'Blue Peter', 548, 76427, 1958, 'A fun-packed and informative magazine show for younger viewers with information and reports from around the world.', 'See also: List of Blue Peter episodes
Blue Peter''s content is wide-ranging. Most programmes are broadcast live, but usually include at least one filmed report. There will also often be a demonstration of an activity in the studio, or a music or dance performance. Between the 1960s and 2011 the programme was made at BBC Television Centre, and often came from Studio 1, the fourth-largest TV studio in Britain and one of the largest in Europe. This enabled Blue Peter to include large-scale demonstrations and performances within the live programme. From the September 2007 series, the programme was broadcast from a small fixed set in Studio 2. However, from 2009 the series began to use the larger studios once more; also more programmes were broadcast in their entirety from the Blue Peter Garden.
The show is also famous for its "makes", which are demonstrations of how to construct a useful object or prepare food. These have given rise to the oft-used phrase "Here''s one I made earlier", as presenters bring out a perfect and completed version of the object they are making – a phrase credited to Christopher Trace, though Marguerite Patten is another possibility. Trace also used the line "And now for something completely different", which was later taken up by Monty Python. Time is also often given over to reading letters and showing pictures sent in by viewers.
Over 5,000 editions have been produced since 1958, and almost every episode from 1964 onwards still exists in the BBC archives. This is unusual for programmes of that era; editor Biddy Baxter personally ensured that telerecordings and, from 1970, video recording were kept of each episode. The earliest surviving footage, a 35mm film sequence, comes from Programme 204 produced and broadcast in 1962. The earliest edition to survive in a complete form is Programme 238 from 1963, which survives as a 16mm film recording.
Many items from Blue Peter''s history have become embedded in British popular culture, especially moments when things have gone wrong, such as the much-repeated clip of Lulu the baby elephant (from a 1969 edition) who urinated and defecated on the studio floor, appeared to tread on the foot of presenter John Noakes and then proceeded to attempt an exit, dragging her keeper along behind her.
Other well-remembered and much-repeated items from this era include a Girl Guides'' campfire that got out of hand on the 1970 Christmas edition, and John Noakes''s report on the cleaning of Nelson''s Column.

^ McGown, Alistair (October 2005). Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, online edn!. Oxford University Press. Trace, Christopher Leonard (1933–1992). Archived from the original on 23 February 2022. Retrieved 29 June 2009.

^ John Ezard (8 October 2005). "Now something different – which was made earlier". The Guardian. London. Archived from the original on 20 September 2014. Retrieved 23 April 2010.

^ "BBC Programme Catalogue". 13 July 2012. Archived from the original on 13 July 2012.{{cite web}}: CS1 maint: deprecated archival service (link)

^ Allfree, Claire (27 April 2025). "Peter Purves on his affair with Valerie Singleton: ''To be fair, it was only one night''". The Telegraph. London. Retrieved 27 April 2025.', 'Returning Series', 'BBC One', '', 7.6, 7, '1958-10-16', '2025-10-17', 6.1341, 'en', 'Blue Peter', 'https://bbc.co.uk/cbbc/shows/blue-peter', 2050, 68, 1, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/qONA6QgXZsqwDNzqv6sLLMicJnU.jpg', '/vooOqBDaPtG0kwhD8h3YLTKLLLF.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:21:46Z', '2026-04-03T17:22:17Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('57bde44b-499e-4477-85db-15ce05ee894e', 'Come Outside', 14343, 71583, 1993, 'A BBC educational children''s television series that aimed to encourage young children to learn about the world around them. The starting point for each programme is something with which children are already familiar, such as water, wood, paper, boots, spiders, buses, soap, street lamps. The two main characters are Auntie Mabel, and her dog Pippin. They go on adventures in Auntie Mabel''s aeroplane, travelling far and wide across the UK to find out more. Music, rhymes and stories enrich the programme topics.', 'The series aims to encourage young children to learn about the world around them. The starting point for each programme is something with which children may already be familiar, such as: wood, paper, boots, spiders, buses, soap and lampposts.
The two main characters are Auntie Mabel (played by Lynda Baron), and her dog Pippin, who was initially played by a female dog also called Pippin. A feature of Come Outside is Auntie Mabel''s unusual mode of transport: a small aeroplane (a Slingsby T67 Firefly) with multi-coloured polka dots.', 'Ended', 'BBC Two', '', 5.8, 5, '1993-09-23', '1997-03-18', 0.3246, 'en', 'Come Outside', 'http://www.bbc.co.uk/cbeebies/comeoutside/', 40, 3, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/6ce4IRSFIg7yoK0OkW3faQF1xlk.jpg', '/mVQa19LBrhqfxmf1Hb3XRAZaYPE.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:19:30Z', '2026-04-03T17:19:35Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('57f0e548-f9d1-412a-8b9b-dc3960a09b22', 'Shaun the Sheep', 3902, 79890, 2007, 'Shaun the Sheep thinks and acts like a person in a barnyard, which usually gets him into trouble. The farmer''s sheepdog, Bitzer, tries to keep Shaun and his friends out of trouble. The farmer is oblivious to the humanlike features of his flock, who are like one big, happy family.', 'Shaun lives with his flock at Mossy Bottom Farm, a small farm in Northern England. Each episode centres around Shaun''s attempts to add excitement to their otherwise boring lives. The action snowballs into fantastic sitcom-style escapades, most often because the sheep are fascinated with human technology and culture. This usually brings them into partnership — and sometimes conflict — with the sheepdog Bitzer, while they all are simultaneously trying to avoid having their anthropomorphism being discovered by the farmer.', 'Returning Series', 'BBC One', '', 7.6, 280, '2007-03-05', '2025-12-01', 13.5559, 'en', 'Shaun the Sheep', 'http://www.shaunthesheep.com', 170, 6, 1, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/z2gPfTQd3I0JLWOAEdKYMQRLoun.jpg', '/onwny8s0VTTN7te9TJnItgvyWHy.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:39:30Z', '2026-04-03T17:39:37Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('5cc5cb2f-38cf-4a64-8a10-9007e89133ed', 'The Phoenix and the Carpet', 26972, 267518, 1997, 'The Phoenix and the Carpet is a six-part British miniseries based on E. Nesbit''s 1904 fantasy novel of the same name. Produced by HIT Entertainment for BBC One, it aired from 16 November to 21 December 1997.

Four Edwardian children find a strange egg in their newly-arrived Persian carpet. It hatches into a Phoenix bird that grants wishes and also transforms the rug into a magic carpet, which takes them on a series of adventures all over the world and at home.', 'Like Nesbit''s The Railway Children, the story begins when a group of children move from London to the countryside of Kent. The five children (Cyril, Anthea, Robert, Jane, and their baby brother, Hilary, known as "the Lamb") are playing in a gravel pit when they uncover a rather grumpy, ugly, and occasionally malevolent Psammead, a sand-fairy with the ability to grant wishes. The Psammead persuades the children to take one wish each day to be shared among them, with the caveat that the wishes will turn to stone at sunset. This, apparently, used to be the rule in the Stone Age, when all that children wished for was food, the bones of which then became fossils. The five children''s first wish is to be "as beautiful as the day." The wish ends at sunset and its effects simply vanish, leading the Psammead to observe that some wishes are too fanciful to be changed to stone.
All the wishes go comically wrong. The children wish to be beautiful, but the servants do not recognise them and shut them out of the house. They wish to be rich, then find themselves with a gravel-pit full of gold spade guineas that no shop will accept as they are no longer in circulation, so they cannot buy anything. A wish for wings seems to be going well, but at sunset the children find themselves stuck on top of a church bell tower with no way down, getting them into trouble with the gamekeeper who must take them home (though this wish has the happy side-effect of introducing the gamekeeper to the children''s housemaid, who later marries him). Robert is bullied by the baker''s boy, then wishes that he was bigger — whereupon he becomes eleven feet tall, and the other children show him at a travelling fair for coins. They also wish themselves into a castle, only to learn that it is being besieged, while a wish to meet real Red Indians ends with the children nearly being scalped.
The children''s infant brother, the Lamb, is the victim of two wishes gone awry. In one, the children become annoyed with tending to their brother and wish that someone else would want him, leading to a situation where everyone wants the baby, and the children must fend off kidnappers and Gypsies. Later, they wish that the baby would grow up faster, causing him to grow all at once into a selfish, smug young man who promptly leaves them all behind.
Finally, the children accidentally wish that they could give a wealthy woman''s jewellery to their mother, causing all the jewellery to appear in their home. It seems that the gamekeeper, who is now their friend, will be blamed for the robbery, and the children must beg the Psammead for a complex series of wishes to set things right. It agrees, on the condition that they will never ask for any more wishes. Only Anthea, who has grown close to It, makes sure that the final wish is that they will meet It again. The Psammead assures them that this wish will be granted.', 'Ended', 'BBC One', '', 0.0, 0, '1997-11-16', '1997-12-21', 1.2376, 'en', 'The Phoenix and the Carpet', '', 6, 1, 0, 'Miniseries', 'GB', '/xtKQuKdj4qQXhnfMb0d25hetu6w.jpg', NULL, '2026-04-03T17:18:34Z', '2026-04-03T17:18:42Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('60191ea0-fcbe-4cef-a923-1cd785f429b3', 'The Puzzle Place', 12954, 78315, 1995, 'The Puzzle Place is an American children''s television series produced by KCET in Los Angeles, California and Lancit Media in New York City, New York. It premiered on the Public Broadcasting Service on January 16, 1995, and ran for about four years, airing its final episode on December 4, 1998. Reruns were continued until March 31, 2000. The show followed a multi-ethnic group of kids from different parts of the United States who hung out at "the Puzzle Place", which is a teen hangout themed around jigsaw puzzle pieces. In each episode the characters were confronted with an everyday conflict usually encountered in childhood and even early teenagerdom, such as making moral decisions, sharing, racism, sexism, etc.', 'The series follows a multi-ethnic group of children (puppets) from different parts of the United States who gather and socialize at a special kids'' hangout area known as the Puzzle Place. In each episode, the characters are confronted with an everyday conflict usually encountered in childhood years, such as making moral decisions, sharing, racism and sexism.', 'Ended', 'PBS Kids', '', 10.0, 1, '1995-01-16', '1998-12-04', 2.3272, 'en', 'The Puzzle Place', '', 75, 3, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/7i58GcVBdMHHOqquMzzLSsMjPFg.jpg', '/yKGbtg5Hpps3BWeIZqPN61hS4Eh.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:12:20Z', '2026-04-03T17:12:26Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('651e32a2-9381-41a0-b21d-16a9261445b8', 'Postman Pat', 1588, 73854, 1981, 'Pat and his black-and-white cat Jess deliver the mail in Greendale.', 'Each episode follows the adventures of Patrick Clifton, a friendly country postman, and his black and white cat Jess, as he delivers the post through the valley of Greendale. Although he initially concentrates on delivering his letters, he nearly always becomes distracted by a concern of one of the villagers and is always keen to help resolve their problems. Notable villagers include the postmistress, Mrs. Goggins; farmer couple Alf and Dorothy Thompson; the Reverend Timms; PC Selby, the police constable; Jeff Pringle, the school teacher; Peter Fogg, a farm hand; George Lancaster, a chicken farmer; Sam Waldron, a grocer with a mobile shop; Miss Hubbard, an upper-class woman; Julia Pottage, who runs Greendale Farm, and Ted Glen, the local handyman and inventor.', 'Ended', 'CBeebies', '', 6.4, 50, '1981-09-16', '2006-03-20', 5.6545, 'en', 'Postman Pat', 'https://www.bbc.co.uk/cbeebies/shows/postman-pat', 106, 5, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/zMlG1lGI2hZjGYU7pFTgEiJgTAn.jpg', '/ini5OKs2Y5pwfyarKgkDBgB3sW9.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:14:29Z', '2026-04-03T17:14:36Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('67529854-9947-41c0-b8d3-417037b58eef', 'Oakie Doke', 3586, 252134, 1995, 'Oakie Doke was a children''s television programme that was broadcast from September 1995 until December 1996 on the BBC. It was produced by Cosgrove Hall Productions and was shown in stop motion animation. The show ran for two series, each containing 14 episodes.', 'Oakie Doke is a character who lives in an oak tree. His head is an acorn and he wears an oak leaf on his upper body. His skin is light green and he has distinctive rosy cheeks. He is very friendly and a well-respected member of the forest. He slides down the slide around his treehouse every morning and his friends include squirrels, mice, frogs, hedgehogs and moles.
In the show''s opening titles, one of Oakie''s friends would ring a bell at the bottom of his tree, which would wake him up. He would then ride a slide that wound round the trunk of the tree to the bottom. The episode then began. In a typical episode, there is a friend who has an everyday problem in the woods, to which Oakie immediately comes to their aid. There is usually a dilemma, but he helps the friend in need and is often assisted by his other friends from the forest. Many of the problems that arise are a result of the actions of Dave and Denzil, who are known to carry actions out without considering the consequences. However, they usually show some remorse when Oakie later confronts the pair about their behaviour.
Towards the end of each episode, after Oakie helps solve the problem, he states: "Well, it''s just like I always say...", followed by a rhyming phrase. This phrase would be in relation to the solution of the problem. This is then greeted with approving laughter and applause from whoever is present at the time.', 'Ended', 'Telemundo', '', 5.0, 2, '1995-09-11', '1998-06-15', 1.6847, 'en', 'Oakie Doke', '', 26, 2, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB,MX,CA,IT', '/6Belgd2PCloaQLhRwpFel9ZJMrx.jpg', NULL, '2026-04-03T17:19:24Z', '2026-04-03T17:19:29Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('67dd0904-9aba-49bc-b1b1-d047b59106ce', 'Kipper', 25985, 71479, 1997, 'Kipper is a charming collection of tales that follows the adventures of a curious and friendly dog as he explores the world, makes new friends, and learns valuable life lessons through imaginative fun and play.', 'The series follows Kipper the Dog as he embarks on various adventures to an assortment of destinations throughout the English countryside. He is joined by friends, Tiger, Pig, Arnold, and Jake.

Kipper (voiced by Martin Clunes) is a fun-loving, caring and warm-hearted dog with a soft voice who is the titular character of the series. He embodies the true essence of what it means to carpe diem. He is also very selfless, even though he hoards lots of small belongings like books and toys. Kipper is a Jack Russell Terrier who has many exciting encounters with his friends.
Tiger (voiced by Chris Lang) is Kipper''s best friend. He is more realistic, practical, and wise than Kipper with a tendency to think long-term. Sometimes Tiger can be very smug and haughty, even though he really is a kind dog at heart. Tiger is a Scottish terrier.
Pig (voiced by Chris Lang) is Kipper''s second best friend. Pig is very outgoing, but loves normality and gets easily irritated by anything strange or mysterious. He likes to eat chocolate cake and biscuits.
Arnold (voiced by Chris Lang) is Pig''s live-in toddler cousin and Kipper''s third best friend. Even though Arnold almost never talks, he is actually more curious, observant, and intelligent than Pig. He can still say easy words like "duck" and "igloo".
Jake (voiced by Chris Lang) is a friendly Old English Sheepdog who is Kipper''s fourth best friend. He is a minor character in the first series who makes more regular appearances after joining the main characters in the second series where he, Kipper, and Tiger have had many adventures together.
The Magic Frog (voiced by Chris Lang) is a magical frog who Kipper met and encountered once.
The Bleeper People (voiced by Chris Lang) are two space aliens who live on the moon.
Mouse (voiced by Julia Sawalha, credited as Julie Sawalha in later appearances) is a young female mouse who lives with Kipper at his house. She is the only female character who appears in the series.', 'Ended', 'Nickelodeon', '', 7.6, 12, '1997-09-05', '2000-12-21', 3.6598, 'en', 'Kipper', '', 78, 6, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/evbqA7XtVJnwJuxdzMqG17nBFqx.jpg', '/28ydoTvksl00rZUP7LheUVS8OHK.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:10:47Z', '2026-04-03T17:10:54Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('69d0907a-f6da-472e-97da-11f5413be48a', 'Fireman Sam', 5200, 73517, 1987, 'Follow the adventures of fireman Sam and his colleagues as they protect the citizens of the Welsh town of Pontypandy. Whenever the alarm sounds, brave Sam and his co-workers can be counted on to jump into a fire engine, hop onto a helicopter, or even launch an inflatable lifeboat to battle blazes, mount rescue missions, or provide medical attention to those in need.', 'The series is set in and around the fictional Welsh town of Pontypandy, a tight-knit community where the local fire service is frequently called upon to handle various emergencies. The show centers on the town''s dependable and highly competent firefighter, Sam Jones, affectionately known by the residents as the "hero next door." Alongside his colleagues at the Pontypandy Fire Station, Sam works tirelessly to keep the townspeople safe from harm. While the early seasons depict Pontypandy as a quaint, rural valley village tucked away in the Welsh mountains, later seasons expanded the setting into a bustling coastal town, allowing the series to broaden the scope of its rescue operations to include maritime and beachside emergencies.

The fire brigade features a regular crew of distinct characters operating under the command of the strict but well-meaning Station Officer Basil Steele. The core team includes Elvis Cridlington, a kind-hearted, guitar-playing firefighter who serves as the station''s cook and often provides comic relief, and Penny Morris, a resourceful mechanic and the station''s first female firefighter. As the series progressed, the team expanded to include newer recruits such as Ellie Phillips and Arnold McKinley, as well as the Australian rescue pilot Tom Thomas, who operates the Mountain Rescue Center. The team utilizes a fleet of specialized rescue vehicles, most notably the primary fire engine, Jupiter, and the smaller, versatile rescue tender, Venus. Over time, the fleet grew to accommodate the town''s coastal and mountainous expansion, introducing the rescue helicopter Wallaby One, the lifeboat Neptune, and various other land and water craft.

Plotlines are heavily driven by the daily activities and misadventures of Pontypandy''s civilian residents. A significant portion of the town''s emergencies are inadvertently caused by Norman Price, a mischievous, red-headed schoolboy with a penchant for skateboarding, superheroes, and practical jokes. Despite the constant supervision of his doting mother, Dilys, who runs the local grocery shop, Norman''s schemes frequently result in fires, cave-ins, or people getting stranded. Other prominent townspeople include Trevor Evans, the local bus driver who occasionally volunteers as an auxiliary firefighter; Mike and Helen Flood, the town handyman and nurse; Bella Lasagne, the cheerful Italian café owner; and Sam''s niece and nephew, the young twins Sarah and James.

Episodes follow a predictable but comforting narrative structure. A typical storyline begins with the townspeople engaging in an everyday activity or community event that quickly goes awry due to carelessness, miscommunication, or minor accidents. Once the danger is realized, the alarm sounds at the fire station, prompting Sam and his team to swiftly deploy. After navigating obstacles to reach the scene, the firefighters execute a rescue and mitigate the hazard. The conclusion of each episode strongly emphasizes educational themes regarding fire safety, emergency preparedness, and the importance of civic responsibility, usually delivered as a gentle reprimand or lesson by Sam. While the original stop-motion animation seasons focused on localized, low-stakes incidents such as kitchen fires, lost animals, or objects stuck in trees, the later computer-animated seasons transitioned into more action-oriented plots, featuring complex cliffside rescues, ocean storms, and large-scale forest fires, though the core prosocial message of community and safety remains intact.', 'Returning Series', 'S4C', '', 6.1, 39, '1987-11-17', '2024-11-12', 21.2322, 'cy', 'Sam Tân', 'http://www.firemansamonline.com', 359, 16, 1, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/sXsqaIOjemMuHxGu3XukIyVM7jN.jpg', '/dEPV4QKCdo8pqLN4g4h8pn6Jga7.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:14:36Z', '2026-04-03T17:15:10Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('6ccee4aa-0ce5-4310-8203-868e44bd835e', 'Katie and Orbie', 10074, 188531, 1993, 'Katie and Orbie is a Canadian animated television series aimed at preschoolers, originally broadcast in Canada from June 2, 1997 by Family Channel and later aired in the United States on PBS from June 2, 1997 and on Disney Channel from June 2, 1997–December 31, 1999. In Canada, the series aired uninterruptedly on Family Channel and (beginning on 2007) Disney Junior (formerly called Playhouse Disney) until December 31, 2012. The series has also aired in several countries around the world.', 'The series revolves around the everyday life and gentle adventures of Katie, an energetic and curious five-year-old girl, and her best friend Orbie, a small, pink alien. Orbie, who originally hails from a distant planet, accidentally crashed his tiny spaceship in Katie''s backyard and was subsequently adopted by her family. Together, the duo lives in a typical suburban home with Katie''s supportive mother and father. Because Orbie is entirely unfamiliar with Earth''s customs, objects, and natural phenomena, the series frequently utilizes his extraterrestrial perspective as a vehicle to explore and explain the ordinary world to a preschool audience. 

A distinctive stylistic feature of the series is its narrative format. Rather than having a cast of voice actors for the individual characters, the show relies almost entirely on voice-over narration, famously provided by actor Leslie Nielsen. Nielsen serves as a gentle, grandfatherly storyteller who narrates the on-screen events, articulates the characters'' internal thoughts and feelings, and guides the audience through the moral of each episode. While Katie is highly expressive through her actions, Orbie communicates exclusively through a series of rhythmic squeaks, beeps, and honks, which Katie, her parents, and their friends inherently understand. The supporting cast is primarily composed of Katie''s parents, neighborhood friends, and various animals, all of whom treat Orbie''s alien nature with total acceptance. 

Episodes typically follow Katie and Orbie as they navigate relatable, everyday childhood milestones and minor challenges, such as going to the doctor, visiting the dentist, learning to share, or coping with common fears like the dark. Because of his origins, Orbie frequently misunderstands ordinary Earth concepts—such as the changing of the seasons, how a camera works, or the purpose of taking a bath—prompting Katie and her parents to patiently explain how these things function. The plotlines deliberately avoid high-stakes conflicts or traditional antagonists, focusing instead on mild obstacles that require communication and teamwork to overcome. 

Based on the series of children''s books illustrated by Canadian cartoonist Ben Wicks and written by his daughter Susan Wicks, the show heavily emphasizes prosocial and educational themes. The narratives consistently highlight empathy, environmental consciousness, problem-solving, and the celebration of differences, using Orbie''s status as a newcomer to teach lessons about inclusion and understanding. Through its soothing pace and storybook framing, the series establishes a comforting, educational tone designed to reassure young viewers while encouraging their natural curiosity about the world around them.', 'Ended', 'PBS', '', 0.0, 0, '1993-05-03', '2003-06-09', 1.3407, 'en', 'Katie and Orbie', '', 78, 6, 0, 'Scripted', 'CA', '/52BNwVLgoqi8bGdOdlV98S93ZIJ.jpg', '/7Kn12mPS7f9tOfvnwX27nzuzjnr.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:11:20Z', '2026-04-03T17:11:47Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('711deee8-75e7-4bec-bbd4-64b017b3b0b8', 'The Real Housewives Ultimate Girls Trip', 136105, 409671, 2021, 'Bravo universes collide as iconic Housewives from different cities come together for the ultimate girls'' trip to beautiful Turks and Caicos.', 'In September 2019, it was announced an untitled The Real Housewives franchise spin-off was in development at Peacock. In February 2021, it was announced Peacock had greenlit a limited series following multiple women from different Housewives installments living together for a period of time.

^ Pederson, Erik (September 17, 2019). "Peacock Programming: Here''s What NBCU''s Streaming Service Will Offer". Deadline Hollywood. Retrieved May 1, 2021.

^ Gugliemi, Jodi (February 25, 2021). "Real Housewives Mashup Series in the Works at NBC''s Peacock". People. Retrieved May 1, 2021.

^ Vena, Jocelyn (April 26, 2021). "The Real Housewives Are Planning the Ultimate Series on Peacock". Bravo. Retrieved May 1, 2021.', 'Returning Series', 'Peacock', 'Real Housewives, Ultimate Drama.', 6.7, 18, '2021-11-15', '2024-01-04', 3.0667, 'en', 'The Real Housewives Ultimate Girls Trip', 'https://www.peacocktv.com/stream-tv/real-housewives-ultimate-girls-trip', 27, 5, 1, 'Reality', 'US', '/iepv1O8RgPREdoWlCaM1zNiUFAm.jpg', '/uGQw3TTAB2eLb6LwBQ4NFwrczcG.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:34:40Z', '2026-04-03T16:34:43Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('74e16096-af9f-436e-a57f-923e0f6239e1', 'Rick and Morty', 60625, 275274, 2013, 'Follows a sociopathic genius scientist who drags his inherently timid grandson on adventures across the universe.', 'See also: List of Rick and Morty characters

Cast members

Justin Roiland

Chris Parnell

Spencer Grammer

Sarah Chalke

Ian Cardoni

Harry Belden

Rick Sanchez, Morty Smith, additional voices(seasons 1–6)

Jerry Smith

Summer Smith

Beth Smith, Space Beth

Rick Sanchez(season 7–present)

Morty Smith(season 7–present)

The show revolves around the adventures of the members of the Smith household, which consists of parents Jerry and Beth, their children Summer and Morty, and Beth''s father, Rick Sanchez, who lives with them as a guest. The family lives in a suburb outside of Seattle, Washington, with the characters traveling to other planets and dimensions through portals and Rick''s flying saucer.
Rick is an eccentric and alcoholic mad scientist, who eschews many ordinary conventions such as school, marriage, love, and family. He frequently goes on adventures with his 14-year-old grandson, Morty, a kind-hearted but easily distressed boy, whose naïve but grounded moral compass plays counterpoint to Rick''s Machiavellian ego. Morty''s 17-year-old sister, Summer, is a more conventional teenager who worries about improving her status among her peers and sometimes follows Rick and Morty on their adventures. The kids'' mother, Beth, is a generally level-headed person and assertive force in the household, though self-conscious about her professional role as a horse surgeon. She is dissatisfied with her marriage to Jerry, a simple-minded and insecure person, who disapproves of Rick''s influence over his family.
Different versions of the characters inhabit other dimensions throughout the show''s multiverse and their personal characteristics can vary from one reality to another. The show''s original Rick identifies himself as "Rick Sanchez of Earth Dimension C-137", in reference to his original universe, but this does not apply to any other member of the Smith household. For instance, in the first-season episode "Rick Potion #9", after turning the entire world population into monsters, Rick and Morty move to a different dimension, leaving the original Summer, Beth and Jerry behind.

^ Roiland, Justin (September 3, 2016). "Washington state- outside of Seattle" (Tweet). Archived from the original on May 20, 2024 – via Twitter.

^ "Rick and Morty series five review – proof that Elon Musk must be stopped!". the Guardian. June 21, 2021. Archived from the original on September 27, 2023. Retrieved September 15, 2022.

^ Kandell, Zachary (March 23, 2020). "Rick & Morty: When Did Rick Stop Burping (& is He Still an Alcoholic?)". Screen Rant. Archived from the original on March 24, 2020.

^ Pulliam-Moore, Charles (September 3, 2021). "Rick and Morty…This Is Heavy". Gizmodo. Archived from the original on September 27, 2023. Retrieved November 21, 2022.

^ Cameron, Charles (January 12, 2022). "Rick & Morty: Why Season 2''s Cob Planet Was So Terrifying To Rick". ScreenRant. Archived from the original on September 27, 2023. Retrieved January 23, 2023.

^ Lebovitz, David L. (November 19, 2020). "How Rick And Morty''s Summer Changed Because Of Spencer Grammer - Exclusive". Looper. Archived from the original on September 27, 2023. Retrieved November 21, 2022.

^ Plante, Corey (December 28, 2020). "''Rick and Morty''s scene-stealing new character will stick around in Season 5". Inverse. Archived from the original on June 28, 2021. Retrieved November 21, 2022.

^ Jung, E. Alex (September 12, 2017). "Here''s What We Know About Rick and Morty''s Evil Morty". Vulture. Archived from the original on September 27, 2023. Retrieved November 21, 2022.

^ Puchko, Kristy (September 5, 2022). "''Rick and Morty'' Season 6 premiere explainer: All your mind-blower questions answered". Mashable. Archived from the original on September 27, 2023. Retrieved November 21, 2022.

^ "Rick And Morty: "Rick Potion #9"". The A.V. Club. January 28, 2014. Archived from the original on August 1, 2021. Retrieved November 21, 2022.', 'Returning Series', 'Adult Swim', 'Science makes sense, family doesn''t.', 8.678, 10769, '2013-12-02', '2025-07-27', 85.0799, 'en', 'Rick and Morty', 'http://www.adultswim.com/videos/rick-and-morty', 91, 9, 1, 'Scripted', 'US', '/WGRQ8FpjkDTzivQJ43t94bOuY0.jpg', '/Ao5pBFuWY32cVuh6iYjEjZMEscN.jpg', '2026-04-03T15:40:40Z', '2026-04-03T16:07:06Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('7de73abb-3d42-4da3-8fd1-5fe548762c95', 'Paddington Bear', 7447, 82747, 1976, 'Paddington Bear is a series of British animated shorts based on the Paddington Bear book series by Michael Bond produced by FilmFair. This was the first television series based on the popular children''s book Paddington Bear. In the United States it was usually shown on pay television as filler in between programs. Its narrator was actor Michael Hordern. It was one of the few television programmes to combine a puppet show with cartoon - Paddington himself was a puppet, but other characters in the series were depicted as cartoon characters.

The series has a very distinctive art style. Paddington himself is a stop-motion animated puppet who moves within a 3-dimensional space and interacts with 2-dimensional animated drawings of the human characters, buildings, etc.

The series, along with all other FilmFair productions is currently owned by DHX Media of Canada.', 'The series follows the everyday misadventures of Paddington, a polite, well-meaning, and anthropomorphic bear from "Darkest Peru." After being discovered at Paddington railway station sitting on a suitcase with a tag that reads "Please look after this bear. Thank you," he is taken in by the middle-class Brown family to live at their home at 32 Windsor Gardens in London. A defining narrative and visual characteristic of the series is its unique mixed-media presentation: Paddington is depicted as a three-dimensional stop-motion puppet wearing his signature bush hat and duffle coat, while the human characters, props, and backgrounds are rendered as two-dimensional, sketch-like paper cutouts. The entire series is narrated by Michael Hordern, who voices all of the characters and provides a comforting, storybook-like atmosphere to the bear''s gentle escapades.

The narrative largely centers on Paddington''s interactions with his adoptive family and his local London community. The Brown household includes the mild-mannered Mr. Brown, his kindly wife Mrs. Brown, their children Jonathan and Judy, and their no-nonsense but warm-hearted housekeeper Mrs. Bird. Outside the home, Paddington frequently visits his closest friend, Mr. Gruber, a wise and gentle antique shop owner with whom he regularly shares "elevenses"—consisting of cocoa and buns—while seeking advice or discussing his latest endeavors. A recurring source of conflict is the Browns'' cantankerous and miserly neighbor, Mr. Curry. Mr. Curry often attempts to trick Paddington into doing free labor or tries to take advantage of the bear''s gullibility, but his schemes inevitably backfire, usually resulting in his own comical misfortune. 

Episodes typically adapt individual chapters from Michael Bond''s original books and follow a predictable comedic structure driven by Paddington''s earnest naivety and clumsiness. Storylines routinely feature Paddington attempting to participate in mundane human activities, such as decorating a room, baking a cake, doing the laundry, or visiting the seaside. His literal interpretations of instructions or unfamiliarity with everyday objects quickly lead to escalating chaos, often resulting in sticky messes—frequently involving his beloved marmalade—or minor property damage. Whenever he is treated unfairly, condescended to, or encounters a particularly rude individual, Paddington is known to deploy his signature "hard stare" to intimidate them into compliance. Despite the localized destruction he inadvertently causes, Paddington''s mishaps almost always resolve fortuitously. He often accidentally catches a thief, wins a contest, or helps a neighbor in need, allowing the episodes to conclude warmly with the bear safely back at home, reflecting on his day''s adventures.', 'Ended', 'BBC One', '', 7.2, 25, '1976-01-05', '1980-04-18', 3.4599, 'en', 'Paddington Bear', 'http://www.paddingtonbear.co.uk/', 56, 2, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/bagwjuYOdgoa4N7JYiAxFvBBxoH.jpg', '/jU0l6b59KkxBmKYP6qUn9fMnLDJ.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:22:51Z', '2026-04-03T17:23:19Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('7f6201f9-072d-4130-8be5-bd20b8d34b0d', 'The Real Housewives of Orange County', 10222, 80117, 2006, 'A view into the lives of several women and their families who live in one of the wealthiest communities in the country with the viewer taken "inside the gates" to show their lives aren''t always perfect.', '*The Real Housewives of Orange County* premiered on March 21, 2006, serving as the inaugural installment of the massive *Real Housewives* franchise. Originally focusing on the exclusive, gated community of Coto de Caza, the first season introduced an ensemble consisting of Kimberly Bryant, Jo De La Rosa, Vicki Gunvalson, Jeana Keough, and Lauri Waring. Early storylines highlighted the women''s extreme wealth, De La Rosa''s reluctance to marry her older fiancé Slade Smiley, Gunvalson''s overbearing work ethic, and Waring''s journey from financial ruin to a lavish wedding to businessman George Peterson. Following Bryant''s early departure, the second and third seasons added Tammy Knickerbocker, Quinn Fry, and Tamra Barney (later Judge). The third season marked a turning point in the show''s tone, steering away from family-centric documentary aesthetics toward high-conflict interpersonal drama, heavily fueled by Judge''s sharp tongue and Keough''s struggling marriage.

The fourth season introduced Gretchen Rossi, whose relationship with her ailing, much older fiancé Jeff drew intense scrutiny from the other women. This skepticism culminated in the infamous "Naked Wasted" incident, where Judge and Gunvalson aggressively encouraged Rossi to consume dangerous amounts of alcohol at a dinner party in an attempt to compromise her fidelity. The fifth and sixth seasons added Alexis Bellino, Lynne Curtin, and Peggy Tanous, while documenting the harsh reality of the 2008 financial crisis hitting Orange County, most notably captured through Curtin''s on-camera eviction. During this era, De La Rosa, Waring, and Keough all departed the series. The show fundamentally shifted in its seventh and eighth seasons with the additions of Heather Dubrow and Lydia McLaughlin. Storylines heavily centered on Judge''s bitter, highly publicized divorce from Simon Barney, Gunvalson''s divorce from her husband Donn, and Rossi''s controversial new romantic relationship with De La Rosa''s ex-fiancé, Slade Smiley. Following a heavily scrutinized rooftop proposal to Smiley, Rossi was let go from the series.

The ninth season introduced Shannon Beador and Lizzie Rovsek, highlighting Beador''s eccentric, holistic lifestyle and the deeply emotional fallout of her husband David''s infidelity. The tenth season added Meghan King Edmonds and was entirely dominated by one of the franchise''s most infamous storylines: the "Cancergate" scandal. The season aggressively chronicled Edmonds''s relentless, meticulous investigation into Gunvalson''s controversial boyfriend, Brooks Ayers, who was ultimately revealed to have fabricated medical records regarding a cancer diagnosis. Gunvalson''s fierce defense of Ayers completely fractured her relationships with the rest of the cast. The eleventh season introduced Kelly Dodd, whose highly volatile, explosive outbursts brought a new level of toxicity to the group, climaxing during a chaotic, alcohol-fueled cast trip to Ireland that alienated her and Gunvalson from the other women. 

Seasons twelve through fourteen saw the departures of Dubrow and Edmonds, alongside the introductions of Peggy Sulahian, Gina Kirschenheiter, Emily Simpson, and Braunwyn Windham-Burke. The narrative was heavily defined by the "Tres Amigas"—the tight-knit, often chaotic alliance of Gunvalson, Judge, and Beador. Storylines focused on Beador''s acrimonious divorce from David, Kirschenheiter''s own divorce and subsequent DUI arrest, and a violent, highly publicized physical altercation between Dodd and Beador involving a mallet. In a massive shift for the franchise, Gunvalson was demoted to a "friend of" role for the fourteenth season, which ended with both Gunvalson and Judge departing the series. The fifteenth season, filmed during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, added Elizabeth Lyn Vargas and deeply chronicled Windham-Burke''s emotional journey of accepting her alcoholism and publicly coming out as a lesbian. Following a severe decline in viewer reception, Bravo initiated a major cast overhaul ahead of the sixteenth season, firing Dodd, Windham-Burke, and Vargas, bringing back Dubrow, and introducing Noella Bergener and Dr. Jen Armstrong.

The seventeenth season featured the highly anticipated return of Judge, alongside newcomer Jennifer Pedranti and Taylor Armstrong, who made history as the first housewife to cross over from another city''s franchise. The season''s narrative centered on Dubrow feeling increasingly isolated by the group and Judge''s aggressive, season-long campaign attacking Beador''s alcohol consumption. Shortly after filming concluded, Beador made national headlines following a shocking arrest for a DUI and hit-and-run, which heavily dictated the plot of the eighteenth season. The eighteenth season added Katie Ginella and was defined by the controversial return of Bellino in a "friend of" capacity. The season captured absolute chaos as Bellino began a highly publicized relationship with Beador''s ex-boyfriend, John Janssen, who simultaneously filed a financial lawsuit against Beador. This dynamic created intensely toxic, deeply fractured alliances within the cast, culminating in Judge viciously turning against her former best friend Beador, leaving the group completely divided.', 'Returning Series', 'Bravo', 'I make my own money, and I make my own rules.', 5.4, 68, '2006-03-21', '2025-11-20', 12.9505, 'en', 'The Real Housewives of Orange County', 'http://www.bravotv.com/the-real-housewives-of-orange-county', 347, 19, 1, 'Reality', 'US', '/mEg3ohdBkLOs0L1GmYGtiwyJXlE.jpg', '/3ZzSaTwUC7wNd9CNTDQ76eP6guG.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:35:28Z', '2026-04-03T16:56:27Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('822343ab-b688-40b9-b089-d0b3ddcdb256', 'Breaking Bad', 1396, 81189, 2008, 'Walter White, a New Mexico chemistry teacher, is diagnosed with Stage III cancer and given a prognosis of only two years left to live. He becomes filled with a sense of fearlessness and an unrelenting desire to secure his family''s financial future at any cost as he enters the dangerous world of drugs and crime.', 'Breaking Bad follows Walter White, a financially struggling, frustrated high school chemistry teacher from Albuquerque, New Mexico, who becomes a crime lord in the local methamphetamine drug trade, driven to provide for his family financially after being diagnosed with inoperable lung cancer. Initially making only small batches of meth with his former student Jesse Pinkman in a rolling meth lab, Walter and Jesse eventually expand to make larger batches of an extremely pure, blue meth which creates high demand. Walter takes on the name "Heisenberg" to mask his identity. Because of his new business, Walter finds himself at odds with his family, the Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) through his brother-in-law Hank Schrader, the local gangs and the Mexican drug cartels (including their regional distributors), putting him and his family''s lives at risk. The events of the series take place between 2008 and 2010.

^ Snierson, Dan (July 17, 2018). "Better Call Saul to show scenes set during Breaking Bad". Entertainment Weekly. Archived from the original on July 17, 2018. Retrieved July 17, 2018.', 'Ended', 'AMC', 'Change the equation.', 8.94, 17422, '2008-01-20', '2013-09-29', 154.9488, 'en', 'Breaking Bad', 'https://www.sonypictures.com/tv/breakingbad', 62, 5, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/ztkUQFLlC19CCMYHW9o1zWhJRNq.jpg', '/tsRy63Mu5cu8etL1X7ZLyf7UP1M.jpg', '2026-04-03T15:40:32Z', '2026-04-03T16:03:29Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('8273a929-000a-4d85-9d00-0bbf6bfde97a', 'Redwall', 29904, 77150, 1999, 'To save his besieged Abbey, a young mouse novice must learn of his destiny to be the successor to a great warrior.', 'Cornflower and Matthias.A young mouse named Matthias lives at Redwall Abbey. Reminiscing on his past life, he remembers how his family was very poor. During a particularly harsh winter, an army of Rats, led by Cluny the Scourge, attacked his village. Separated from his family, Matthias saw Cluny just before he was rescued by his sister and, with advice from voles, travelled to Redwall Abbey, with the memory of Cluny still fresh in their minds. After difficult travels, Matthias'' sister collapsed, and Matthias followed soon after. When he came to, he found that he had been brought to Redwall Abbey, where the fathers of the abbey later informed him that his sister had died of exhaustion from the journey. Matthias was then raised in the abbey as a novice, yet dreamed of becoming a great warrior like Martin, the warrior-mouse that helped found Redwall. When he joined Constance the Badger in escorting some woodlanders from a feast at the Abbey, he was shocked when he spotted Cluny and his rats marching onto Redwall, taking a nearby church as their main base. Cluny and his captains went to Redwall, claiming to be travelers until they were inside, when Cluny makes his demands clear. In a fit of rage, Matthias attacked them, telling them exactly what he thought of them. Cluny and his captains were forced to leave when Constance threatened to kill them if they did not leave. When he was forced outside, Cluny instructed his only climber, the ninja-esque Shadow, to steal the image of Martin from the tapestry to reduce Redwall''s morale. Shadow was successful, but was intercepted by Matthias, and fell to his death from the walls. Cluny then took the image of Martin as his war standard. However, Cluny had nightmares about Martin and Matthias for as long as the tapestry was in his possession. This was soon remedied when Matthias''s friends, Jess Squirrel and Basil Stag Hare, stole the tapestry back.
After a number of battles, Matthias was busily looking for Martin''s sword, theorizing that they could drive Cluny away if they found it. With old Methuselah the abbey recorder and Cornflower (a young mouse who Matthias was close friends with), they discover that Matthias was to be the next Abbey warrior, like Martin before him. Through a riddle they found under the tapestry, they discovered Martin''s tomb underneath a set of stone stairs, and there they found Martin''s shield and sword-belt. Next, Matthias found Martin''s sheath in the roost of the Sparra, a tribe of savage sparrows living in the roof spaces, ruled by the insane King Bull Sparra. Matthias stole the sheath from the Sparra, killing Bull Sparra in the process, and befriended a young Sparrow named Warbeak. However, he learned that the sword was taken from the Sparra by Asmodeus Poisonteeth, a huge snake. On a journey to find information, Matthias met the Guosim Shrews, a tribe of argumentative shrews who told him to speak to a large snowy owl named Captain Snow for information. Snow told him that Asmodeus lives in a nearby quarry, but mocked Matthias for thinking he could stand a chance against the snake. In fact, he bet Matthias that if he got the sword, he would give up eating mice (and shrews), as well as apologise to Julian Gingivere, a vegetarian cat who used to be friends with Snow, until he banned Snow from his home because of his manners and taste in food. Matthias, after a brief return to the Abbey, set off on an expedition to find the sword, with help from the Guosim. They followed Asmodeus to the large quarry. There, Matthias found the sword, and in a fierce battle with the snake, decapitated him. Meanwhile, Cornflower discovered Martin''s old battle-armour in an abbey attic.
Throughout this period, Cluny had been using various tactics to try to take over Redwall, including tunnelling in, using a battering ram, blackmailing a spice-merchant dormouse to kidnap Cornflower as ransom, burning the gates, using a siege tower, and sneaking his soldiers in with a visiting local circus. Every single time the rats failed, and Cluny began to show signs of insanity.
While Matthias was away gathering more troops to fight the rats, Cluny forced a dormouse he had captured to sneak into Redwall and open the gates from the inside. Cluny and his army entered the Abbey at last. However, Matthias came back, dressed in Martin''s armour, alongside an army of shrews and sparrows that he had gathered. He found that Cluny had taken over, and was about to execute all the Redwallers (starting with Cornflower). The sight of Matthias dressed as the mouse he had seen in his dreams terrified Cluny, who sent his troops against Matthias and his army. During the ensuing battle between Cluny''s army and the mixed defenders of Redwall (The Defenders of Redwall, the Guosim, and the Sparra), Cluny kidnapped Cornflower and hid in the belltower. When Matthias followed them in, Cluny ambushed him, and a duel began, which led them both up to the top of the belltower. Cluny jumped down and caught Cornflower again, threatening to kill her if Mathias did not come down to face him. Mathias swore to, if Cluny released her. Cluny released Cornflower, but Matthias cut the bellropes, causing the Abbey-bell to crash down onto Cluny, killing him instantly.
In the aftermath of the battle, Abbot Mortimer was fatally wounded. Before he died, he declared Matthias to be the Abbey warrior, and that Cornflower would be his wife.
In the epilogue, we see how life in Redwall is back to normal. The Sparra are now ruled by Warbeak who is a good ruler, and the Sparra are now friends with the Redwallers. Some of the Guosim Shrews have chosen to stay at Redwall, and have become beekeepers (even learning to speak to the bees, so they can argue with them). The new abbot is brother Mordalfus, previously known as brother Alf, who used to run the Abbey Pond. Matthias and Cornflower are now happily married and have a son, named Mattimeo (which is somehow short for Matthias Methuselah Mortimer (possibly MATThIas MEthuselah mOrtimer)). The new Abbey Recorder, John Churchmouse, signs off saying that the gates of Redwall are always open to travellers, inviting the viewer to visit if they are ever passing.

Several seasons after the death of Cluny, Matthias and Cornflower had a son, Mattimeo. But one night, as the Redwallers were celebrating, they were interrupted by the masked fox, Slagar the Cruel, who was previously known as Chickenhound, who entered Redwall with a band of rodents, drugged everyone and kidnapped all of their children. Matthias, Basil Stag Hare and Jess headed out to save them, gaining new and old allies along the way, such as Orlando the Axe, the Guosim shrews, and the "Sparra".
Meanwhile, in their absence, things started to go wrong for Redwall. It came under attack by ravens, led by General Ironbeak. The ravens took all of their food and the dormitories, leaving Cavern Hole as the only free location. However, the Redwallers struck back by using Martin''s armor as a ghost to scare the raven. They later saved a mountain bird named Stryk Redkite, who was being harassed and later hurt by the ravens. Eventually, Ironbeak figured out their little trick after seeing Constance the badger going to put away Martin''s armour. He locked her in the gatehouse and had his troops take Cavern Hole. Constance was able to break out and save the Redwallers as a newly recovered Stryk killed Ironbeak. The other ravens left Redwall.
Matthias and company were able to follow Slagar''s trail to an old buried abbey, Loamhedge, where a cult of rats who worshiped Malkariss, were enslaving others. The fight ended with the slaves stoning Malkariss to death and Matthias setting them free. Eventually, they won with the abbey sinking into the earth. At the surface, Slagar tried to get back at Matthias for the death of his mother but ended up falling into the hole from which he escaped.
Company returned to Redwall with the young ones and slaves. After seven seasons, things were going peacefully at Redwall. The slaves of Malkariss and Matthias'' companions settled in Redwall. Matthias, Basil and Orlando began training Redwall''s next generation of defenders.

At the same seventh season after Matthias returned, the Redwallers listen to Tim Churchmouse, who tells the tale of how their hero, Martin, became the warrior he is known as today. In the lands of the Marshank coast, the terrible Badrang the Tyrant was increasing his piracy attacks, forcing Luke the Warrior, Martin''s father, to go out to sea to combat the sea rats. He left his son his sword and told him to never let another creature take it. Years later, Martin was captured by Badrang and his sword taken. The young mouse served many years in the fortress of Marshank as a slave. When he defended an old squirrel named Barkjon, Badrang had him hung outside in the rain with hungry gulls to peck at him and was later put in the prison pit with Barkjon''s son, Felldoh, and a mouse named Brome. Meanwhile, Badrang''s old shipmate, Tramun Clogg, arrived to take Marshank. Brome''s sister, Laterose, also known as Rose, and her friend, the mole Grumm, also arrived and began digging their way into the prison pit while Clogg began his siege. The five made it to Clogg''s boats and managed to commandeer one. However, the boat had a hole, flooding it. A raging storm and a fish separated the group. Martin, Rose and Grumm ended up captured by pygmy shrews and met a hedgehog by the name of Pallum. After saving Queen Amballa''s son, Dinjer, they were free to go to Noonvale, Rose''s home.
Meanwhile, Brome and Felldoh washed up somewhere else, meeting the Rosehip Players, a traveling circus who agreed to help them free the slaves of Marshank. Managing to win Clogg''s amusement, they got inside Marshank and freed most of the slaves. A few days later, Brome disguised himself as one of the rats to get inside, and freed the other slaves through the prison pit''s tunnel.
En route to Noonvale, the company made many friends, such as the Warden of Marshwood Hill and Boldred the owl and enemies like the uncivilized cannibalistic lizards and the rogue Gawtrybe. With help from the shrews and otters, they made it to Noonvale but were unable to raise an army to defeat Badrang. However, many were drawn to Martin''s cause, including the Gawtrybe as he made his way to Marshank.
Clogg took his opportunity to capture Marshank while Badrang was out. However, Badrang knew of the tunnel in the prison pit. He had Clogg''s soldiers swear allegiance to him while Clogg became Marshank''s one man slave operation. Felldoh became so obsessed with Marshank''s fall that he began solo attacks against it. When he faced Badrang head-to-head, he was beaten to death by his minions. It wasn''t until Martin arrived with his army that he forced Badrang into a corner. Martin''s army burned down Marshank''s gate, and began their attack. In the confusion, Martin retrieved his father''s sword and slew Badrang with it. Unfortunately, Badrang had stabbed and killed Rose during the battle. The Fur and Freedom Fighters returned to Noonvale, where it will remain a secret, while Martin continued on his own path.', 'Ended', '5', '', 7.3, 23, '1999-09-08', '2002-02-25', 4.939, 'en', 'Redwall', 'http://www.redwalltv.com/', 39, 3, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB,CA,FR', '/vLXvgV2bpqPuva4k0jCSJ4kidRZ.jpg', '/iunTZGVT3HMhsM331YRYtOWcgtW.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:12:07Z', '2026-04-03T17:12:13Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('85e902a6-b5e3-4f77-b6fd-b3e2b62d2f06', 'Sesame Street', 502, 78419, 1969, 'On a special inner city street, the inhabitants—human and muppet—teach preschoolers basic educational and social concepts using comedy, cartoons, games, and songs.', 'The series is set on Sesame Street, a fictional, diverse inner-city neighborhood in New York City populated by humans, anthropomorphic animals, and colorful creatures known as Muppets. Designed as an educational program for preschoolers, the show blends live-action neighborhood scenes with animated segments, short documentary films, and musical numbers. The narrative explores fundamental academic concepts, such as letters, numbers, and shapes, alongside social and emotional lessons. The community operates as a safe, utopian micro-society where people and monsters of all shapes, sizes, and backgrounds coexist harmoniously and help one another navigate the daily challenges of growing up.

The emotional core of the series is often anchored by Big Bird, an eight-foot-two yellow bird who acts as a surrogate for the preschool audience, asking questions and learning about the world with childlike innocence. He is frequently accompanied by his best friend, Mr. Snuffleupagus, a large woolly mammoth-like creature. The neighborhood is home to a wide variety of distinctive Muppet characters, each utilizing comedic exaggeration to teach specific concepts or behaviors. These include Bert and Ernie, an "odd couple" pair of roommates whose contrasting personalities—Ernie being playful and mischievous, Bert being serious and easily frustrated—drive much of the show''s character-based comedy; Oscar the Grouch, a cynical, garbage-loving monster who lives in a trash can and teaches children about differing perspectives; Cookie Monster, a voracious, googly-eyed creature who frequently struggles with self-regulation; Grover, a well-meaning but bumbling monster who takes on various jobs; Count von Count, a friendly vampire obsessed with counting; and Elmo, an energetic furry red monster who became a central figure in later seasons, often leading his own dedicated segments.

The Muppets interact seamlessly with a diverse, multi-generational cast of human characters who serve as grounded mentor and parental figures. Long-running human residents include the married couples Gordon and Susan, and Luis and Maria, as well as the music teacher Bob, and the gruff but warmhearted shopkeeper Mr. Hooper. The neighborhood revolves around key communal locations, most notably the 123 Sesame Street brownstone building, where many of the human residents live, and Hooper''s Store, a local bodega and lunch counter that serves as a central gathering place. These humans help guide the Muppet characters—and by extension, the viewers—through everyday situations, mediating conflicts and explaining complex social dynamics.

Episodes are traditionally structured around "Street Scenes"—a continuing narrative thread set on the main neighborhood set—interspersed with standalone sketches, celebrity guest appearances, and parodies of popular culture. These segments are famously framed as being "sponsored" by specific letters of the alphabet and numbers. While the series focuses heavily on foundational early childhood education, it also dedicates significant narrative attention to real-world, life-cycle events. Throughout its run, the show has tackled major developmental and societal themes by integrating them directly into the plot, such as the birth of Luis and Maria''s daughter Gabi, the marriage of various characters, and, most notably, teaching children about the concept of grief and death following the real-life passing of Will Lee, the actor who played Mr. Hooper. As the series progressed across decades, the narrative format evolved to include dedicated character blocks and expanded its focus to encompass broader issues like autism, foster care, and emotional resilience, while maintaining its core mission of using comedy and compassion to foster learning.', 'Returning Series', 'PBS', 'Kindness is in the air.', 7.1, 279, '1969-11-10', '2026-03-09', 63.9376, 'en', 'Sesame Street', 'http://www.sesamestreet.org', 3543, 56, 1, 'Scripted', 'US', '/14k9BfZ2p4rQBMeJ5crKTfUZVwD.jpg', '/tNi2aJPdCKfielGYzIi3IKxStz9.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:36:27Z', '2026-04-03T17:37:19Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('8639998c-d0ba-4c81-848a-d2088456b21b', 'The Real Housewives of Dubai', 196592, 413388, 2022, 'As the 11th city in the franchise, the series highlights a group of women navigating their relationships, careers, and supremely lavish and ultra-wealthy lifestyles in the United Arab Emirates. With the desert paradise of Dubai as the backdrop, this billionaire''s playground is known for its over-the-top opulence, jaw-dropping modern architecture and wild nightlife scene. Whether they''re coasting on a private plane, running their own empires or hosting on a private island, these glamourous, successful women are ready to serve up scorching hot drama and unexpected twists.', 'The series premiered as Bravo''s first original international installment, introducing an affluent ensemble residing in the United Arab Emirates consisting of Nina Ali, Chanel Ayan, Caroline Brooks, Dr. Sara Al Madani, Lesa Milan, and Caroline Stanbury. The first season focused on the women''s opulent lifestyles and business ventures within the luxurious, highly exclusive social circles of Dubai. Early storylines centered heavily on former *Ladies of London* star Caroline Stanbury as she prepared for her lavish wedding to much younger former soccer player Sergio Carrallo. Her assertive personality quickly caused friction with Chanel Ayan, a prominent Kenyan-born supermodel known for her extravagant fashion and highly unfiltered commentary. Ayan found a staunch ally in Jamaican fashion designer Lesa Milan, creating a distinct divide within the group. Beyond the interpersonal drama, the season provided deeply emotional moments, most notably when Ayan candidly revealed her childhood trauma of surviving female genital mutilation. Meanwhile, Emirati entrepreneur Dr. Sara Al Madani frequently dispensed unsolicited holistic and parenting advice, sparking tensions with Caroline Brooks, a fiercely independent real estate director working to open an inclusive luxury spa. Amidst the clashes, fruitcake business owner Nina Ali largely attempted to act as the neutral peacemaker, though she often found herself caught in the crossfire between Stanbury and the rest of the cast.

Following the first season, Ali departed the series, and fitness entrepreneur Taleen Marie was introduced as a new main cast member for the second season. The group''s interpersonal dynamics underwent a massive shift, marked most notably by the unexpected reconciliation and budding friendship between former rivals Ayan and Stanbury. This new alliance caused significant strain on Ayan''s previously inseparable, ride-or-die bond with Milan. Their relationship rapidly deteriorated into a bitter, season-long fallout over perceived betrayals, business disputes, and leaked voice notes, which ultimately shattered their friendship by the time of the season''s reunion. Marie, who was initially introduced through her long-standing friendship with Brooks, also found herself at odds with her sponsor. Their relationship collapsed into explosive confrontations as Brooks accused Marie of being opportunistic and disloyal, while Marie criticized Brooks'' increasingly erratic behavior and drinking. Elsewhere, Stanbury and Carrallo navigated the massive stresses of moving into a newly constructed luxury home while heavily debating the prospect of expanding their family via surrogacy. The season also chronicled Brooks'' stressful final preparations to successfully open her Glass House salon, as well as Al Madani''s intense, albeit short-lived, long-distance romance with a German businessman.', 'Canceled', 'Bravo', 'If you think money can’t buy you happiness, you clearly haven’t been to Dubai.', 6.2, 11, '2022-06-01', '2024-09-17', 1.9185, 'en', 'The Real Housewives of Dubai', 'https://www.bravotv.com/the-real-housewives-of-dubai', 29, 2, 0, 'Reality', 'US', '/nC8PyRTFwrs6xKDrGSJWoqcYBUR.jpg', '/8kGaLkIdPWly8GJTZtzZBNsSwQ7.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:35:07Z', '2026-04-03T16:54:16Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('87af6181-d36d-4591-87c2-33400a5f013c', 'The Middle', 1422, 95021, 2009, 'The daily mishaps of a married woman and her semi-dysfunctional family and their attempts to survive life in general in the city of Orson, Indiana.', 'The series features Frances "Frankie" Heck (Patricia Heaton), a middle-class, middle-aged and Midwestern woman and her husband Mike (Neil Flynn), who reside in the small fictional town of Orson, Indiana, based on the real town of Jasper, Indiana. They are the parents of three children, 15-year-old Axl (Charlie McDermott), 13-year-old Sue (Eden Sher) and 8-year-old Brick (Atticus Shaffer).
The series is narrated by Frankie, initially an under-performing salesperson at a used-car dealership and later a dental assistant. Her stoic husband Mike manages a local quarry and serves as a stabilizing influence in the family, though Frankie complains about his lack of affection at times. The children are quite different from one another: eldest son Axl, a stereotypical jock, is outgoing and popular at school but is quite rude and self-centered; daughter Sue is an enthusiastic and perky young girl but is chronically unsuccessful and awkward; and youngest son Brick is an intelligent, compulsive reader with odd behavioral traits. Other characters include Frankie''s co-worker Bob (main character in the first two seasons), Frankie''s elderly Aunt Edie, Sue''s first boyfriend Brad, Mike''s brother Rusty and Axl''s friends Sean and Darrin, who both had a relationship with Sue.

^ "ABC''s ''The Middle'' Loaded With Hoosier Connections". RTV6. May 9, 2012. Archived from the original on December 20, 2016. Retrieved December 8, 2016.

^ "ABC Puts Patricia Heaton in ''The Middle''". Zap2it. November 19, 2008. Archived from the original on January 14, 2010. Retrieved January 17, 2010.', 'Ended', 'ABC', 'Oh, and your family''s so perfect.', 7.6, 729, '2009-09-30', '2018-05-22', 54.0239, 'en', 'The Middle', 'http://www.abc.go.com/shows/the-middle', 215, 9, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/u6FluiFx3ocVS0MgZszfpPKXT2p.jpg', '/82D01BlixH1JD0BCQoERabDjp7h.jpg', '2026-04-03T15:40:28Z', '2026-04-03T16:03:25Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('8a03ad48-ae19-48cf-9321-3b1e3eb34783', 'Adventures from the Book of Virtues', 4434, 76598, 1996, 'Growing up can be confusing! When Zach and Annie face difficulties in their lives, they go to special place called Plato’s Peak. There, Plato—a talking buffalo!—and his friends are always willing to lend a helping hand.', 'The series centered on two best friends: 10-year-old Annie Redfeather, who is Native American, and 11-year-old Zach Nichols, who is white. In each episode of the series, one of them commits an act contrary to that day''s chosen virtue (loyalty, compassion, courage, moderation, honesty, etc.) and suffers pain as a result (be it physical or moral). They seek counsel of one of Annie''s animal friends. These animal friends are four anthropomorphic mountain-dwelling entities who between them possess immense knowledge of legends and literature as well as common sense and a lively sense of fun. They utilize classical works of famous authors, philosophers, poets, as well as fables and myths to communicate the truth of virtue to Zach and Annie. Plato, the oldest, is a scholarly bison; Aurora, the most gentle, is a red-tailed hawk; Socrates "Sock" is a rambunctious bobcat; and Aristotle "Ari" is a prairie dog who is seldom without his bag of books. These four, whose existence seems a secret from the majority of humans in the town of Spring Valley, advise Annie and Zach patiently and often. The children then proceed to live according to the virtue of the day, completing what they have begun.', 'Ended', 'PBS Kids', '', 8.2, 6, '1996-09-02', '2000-12-17', 10.9388, 'en', 'Adventures from the Book of Virtues', 'http://www.pbs.org/adventures', 39, 3, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/sqgKMcufQdP3NVvnrXeIuwzEKrv.jpg', '/lN0jkI1XbN7NVSpfktmLnwnqrag.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:38:33Z', '2026-04-03T17:38:39Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('8e92650a-c8be-4461-ad47-7c8e4e7028ae', 'Old Bear Stories', 4737, 75500, 1993, 'In a playroom which is home to a variety of sentient toys, their leader, the wise and caring "Old Bear" had disappeared some time ago, having been put into the loft and forgotten. After the toys rescue him and bring him back down to the playroom, Old Bear again becomes their most respected toy. Each episode focuses on the adventures of Old Bear and his loveable friends in and about the playroom.', 'In a playroom that is home to a variety of sentient toys, their wise and caring leader, Old Bear, has long been absent after being put into the loft and forgotten. After the toys rescue him and bring him back to the playroom, Old Bear once again becomes their most respected toy and guides his friends in their many adventures, both in the playroom and in the garden.', 'Ended', 'ITV1', '', 8.5, 3, '1993-10-03', '1997-12-24', 1.2608, 'en', 'Old Bear Stories', '', 41, 3, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/r4VBugitcQFSfgnzJSWICFzkY1k.jpg', '/6CTJdIH7jTdQ8iRvK4iaZOiY1WX.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:18:55Z', '2026-04-03T17:19:00Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('8fa41b21-585a-4283-b31d-be7c4f46ed32', 'Jackanory', 8861, 72645, 1965, 'Jackanory is a long-running BBC children''s television series that was designed to stimulate an interest in reading. The show was first transmitted on 13 December 1965, the first story being the fairy-tale Cap-o''-Rushes read by Lee Montague. Jackanory continued to be broadcast until 1996, clocking up around 3,500 episodes in its 30-year run. The final story, The House at Pooh Corner by A. A. Milne, was read by Alan Bennett and broadcast on 24 March 1996. The show returned on 27 November 2006 for two one-off stories.

The show''s format, which varied little over the decades, involved an actor reading from children''s novels or folk tales, usually while seated in an armchair. From time to time the scene being read would be illustrated by a specially commissioned still drawing, often by Quentin Blake. Usually a single book would occupy five daily fifteen-minute episodes, from Monday to Friday.', 'A standard broadcast of the series consists of a prominent actor or public figure reading a children''s novel, folk tale, or myth directly to the television audience. The format is typically highly minimalist, focusing primarily on the storyteller''s vocal performance and expressive delivery to captivate the viewer. The reader is generally seated in a comfortable armchair within a simple studio set, which is sometimes dressed with props or minimal scenery to reflect the theme or setting of the story being told. A single book or story is usually divided into five 15-minute episodes, broadcast daily from Monday to Friday, creating a week-long serialization of the narrative. 

As the narrator reads, the program frequently cuts away from the studio to display specially commissioned still illustrations that depict the characters and events being described. These artworks were contributed by prominent artists and children''s book illustrators, such as Quentin Blake and Shirley Hughes. The camera often pans or zooms across these still illustrations to add a sense of motion and visual interest to the broadcast. While later episodes occasionally incorporated more elaborate visual techniques, on-location filming, or minor dramatizations, the core focus always remained on the solo narrator delivering the text.

Because the stories change weekly, there is no recurring cast of characters or ongoing plotline. Instead, the series features a wide variety of literature, ranging from traditional fairy tales to contemporary children''s books, with the explicit goal of encouraging children to read. The program relies heavily on its rotating roster of guest storytellers, and over the course of its run, hundreds of notable actors, comedians, and public figures served as readers. Notable storytellers have included Bernard Cribbins, Kenneth Williams, Alan Bennett, Rik Mayall, and King Charles III (then Prince of Wales), who read his own specially written children''s story, *The Old Man of Lochnagar*.', 'Returning Series', 'BBC One', '', 6.0, 1, '1965-12-13', '1991-01-04', 1.9023, 'en', 'Jackanory', '', 87, 5, 1, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/kN6IhcoR2FGKCERFG9BV5Dfko3O.jpg', NULL, '2026-04-03T17:20:11Z', '2026-04-03T17:20:33Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('90d490fa-c5ec-40a5-aac3-c70e29b32b31', 'Sister Wives', 34045, 193711, 2010, 'Husband Kody Brown, along with his four wives (only one of which is legally married to Kody) and their combined 18 children, attempts to navigate life as a "normal" family in a society that shuns their lifestyle.', '*Sister Wives* follows the daily life of Kody Brown, a polygamist, his four wives—Meri, Janelle, Christine, and Robyn—and their combined 18 children. Initially residing in a single shared home in Lehi, Utah, the family belongs to the Apostolic United Brethren (AUB), a fundamentalist Mormon group. The series was originally conceived to document and destigmatize the practice of plural marriage, showing how the Browns navigate the complex logistical, financial, and emotional challenges of their lifestyle in a modern society. At the onset of the series, only Kody''s first marriage to Meri is legally recognized, while his subsequent unions with Janelle, Christine, and Robyn are considered "spiritual marriages" by the family. 

The early seasons chronicle the family''s adjustment to the integration of Robyn, Kody''s first new wife in sixteen years, and her three children from a previous marriage. Shortly after Robyn joins the family, the Browns become the subjects of a police investigation by the state of Utah regarding their polygamist lifestyle. Fearing criminal prosecution, the family abruptly uproots and moves to Las Vegas, Nevada. After initially struggling with physical separation in different rental homes, they eventually build four adjacent houses in a cul-de-sac, achieving a period of stability and unity. A major structural shift within the family occurs in 2014 when Kody legally divorces Meri in order to legally marry Robyn. This legal restructuring is done so that Kody can formally adopt Robyn''s children, but it introduces deep emotional complications among the wives.

In the subsequent seasons, relationships within the plural marriage begin to severely fracture. Feeling isolated following her legal divorce and the departure of her only child for college, Meri seeks emotional connection online and becomes the victim of a highly publicized "catfishing" scandal, in which she is manipulated by a woman posing as a male admirer. The fallout from the scandal deeply damages her intimacy with Kody and strains her ties with the rest of the family. Seeking a change of pace and capitalizing on a booming housing market, Kody later convinces the family to move to Flagstaff, Arizona, where they purchase a large plot of undeveloped land known as Coyote Pass. However, the move forces the wives to live miles apart in separate residences. The physical distance causes daily communication to break down, isolating the family members from one another as they struggle with the financial burdens of developing the new property.

The family''s underlying marital cracks are ultimately blown open by the COVID-19 pandemic. Kody implements extremely strict social distancing protocols that he demands the entire family follow. Janelle and Christine find the rules to be excessive, noting that they are damaging to the mental health of their older children and preventing the family from gathering. Because Kody refuses to visit family members who do not strictly adhere to his protocols, he spends the vast majority of the pandemic living exclusively at Robyn''s house. This leads to bitter conflicts and open accusations from the other wives and children that Kody favors Robyn and neglects the rest of the family. The mounting resentment and unequal treatment culminate in the complete collapse of the plural marriage. Christine officially announces her separation from Kody and relocates to Utah. Following her departure, deep-seated conflicts lead Janelle to separate from Kody as well, and Kody and Meri permanently terminate their spiritual marriage, effectively leaving Kody in a monogamous relationship with Robyn.', 'Returning Series', 'TLC', '', 6.0, 51, '2010-09-26', '2026-02-01', 14.5429, 'en', 'Sister Wives', 'https://tlc.com/tv-shows/sister-wives/', 274, 17, 1, 'Reality', 'US', '/lnSXPiP9nYx23AbaekTCdTBq722.jpg', '/bpD0l55NTyHzIGX0QdTdogTk1ro.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:34:26Z', '2026-04-03T16:52:57Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('96231e09-e67e-4ba0-945d-261fcd60faed', 'Cocomelon', 114718, 373184, 2020, 'JJ and his pals sing and dance their way through fun adventures as they learn about letters, numbers, and more.', 'Cocomelon''s videos, for a target audience of two- to five-year-olds, feature 3D animated children, adults, and animals who interact with each other in daily life. The lyrics appear at the bottom of the screen in the same way on all displays. Formats include standalone music videos, compilations, and livestreams. A blend of live action and animation was introduced in 2024 in one of the shows. The toddler characters'' ages ''stretch'' to reflect both the reality and the aspiration of their audience''s lives," according to the Cocomelon creative staff. There is a proprietary staff-only guide of about 100 pages detailing the Cocomelon backstory and characters.

^ "In a breakthrough year for content, multicultural audiences crown ''Cocomelon'' and ''Moana'' among the most streamed titles ever". Nielsen Company. February 2022. Archived from the original on September 24, 2024. Retrieved September 23, 2024.

