^ Cite error: The named reference newyorker/Captures was invoked but never defined (see the help page).', 'Returning Series', 'YouTube', '', 6.5, 37, '2020-05-31', '2024-09-16', 5.8946, 'en', 'Cocomelon', 'https://cocomelon.com/', 88, 12, 1, 'Scripted', 'US,GB', '/7gliOqwY7YwFAxrjfMVcryuvnW.jpg', '/zTDwrUS5ZjS20swYerIJR8cFqwv.jpg', '2026-04-03T15:26:05Z', '2026-04-03T16:10:14Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('99da6080-0787-4529-bf4c-c8f2ea766b3f', 'Franklin', 1458, 78150, 1997, 'Franklin is an Canadian educational animated television series, based on the Franklin the Turtle books by Brenda Clark and Paulette Bourgeois. The television series was named after its main character, Franklin the Turtle. It was produced by PolyGram Television, Alphanim, LuxAnimation, Nelvana, Neurones Enterprises, Reader''s Digest for Young Families, TF1, Funbag Animation Studios, Europool, Mini TFO, and Family Channel, and syndicated by Summit Entertainment.', 'Franklin follows the eponymous young anthropomorphic turtle who lives in a small village called Woodland with his friends. His television stories and books always begin, " could count by twos and tie his shoes". Episodes of the series follow Franklin''s adventures as he goes to school, plays, learns and grows in the world around him, sometimes with the helping hand of an adult such as his parents, Mr. and Mrs. Turtle.', 'Ended', 'CBC Television', '', 6.7, 111, '1997-11-03', '2004-08-08', 9.8346, 'en', 'Franklin', 'http://franklin.treehousetv.com/', 156, 6, 0, 'Scripted', 'CA', '/ep0ol2okz8IB8T0LxhfJMXvJ3Mk.jpg', '/yWHVK0Gswv2mH8V7fsJ2wshBJRO.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:40:14Z', '2026-04-03T17:40:21Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('9d3d37fb-8707-42fa-a874-df8d61e4f530', 'Mister Rogers'' Neighborhood', 15, 77750, 1968, 'Mister Rogers'' Neighborhood is an American children''s television series that was created and hosted by namesake Fred Rogers. The series originated in 1963 as Misterogers on CBC Television, and was later debuted in 1966 as Misterogers'' Neighborhood on the regional Eastern Educational Network, followed by its US network debut on February 19, 1968, and it aired on NET and its successor, PBS, until August 31, 2001. The series is aimed primarily at preschool ages 2 to 5, but has been stated by PBS as "appropriate for all ages". Mister Rogers'' Neighborhood was produced by Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, USA public broadcaster WQED and Rogers'' non-profit production company Family Communications, Inc.; previously known as Small World Enterprises prior to 1971, the company was renamed The Fred Rogers Company after Rogers'' death.', 'Every episode begins with Fred Rogers entering his television house, singing the show''s theme song, "Won''t You Be My Neighbor?". As he sings, he speaks directly to the viewing audience, hangs up his suit jacket, puts on a zip-up cardigan sweater, takes off his dress shoes, and ties on a pair of canvas sneakers. Rogers then introduces the day''s theme, which often centers on early childhood social and emotional development. During this realistic first half of the program, Rogers might demonstrate a craft, play music, or interact with visiting guests. A frequent visitor is Mr. McFeely, the neighborhood delivery man for the "Speedy Delivery" service, who regularly brings physical items or videotapes. These tapes are viewed on "Picture Picture," a wall-mounted screen that displays documentary-style short films showing how everyday objects—such as crayons, trumpets, or macaroni—are manufactured.

Midway through the broadcast, the focus shifts to the "Neighborhood of Make-Believe," a fantastical realm inhabited by both live-action and puppet characters. The transition is marked by the Neighborhood Trolley, a motorized miniature trolley that travels along a track in Rogers'' house, accompanied by a distinct piano motif, and passes through a tunnel to enter the magical kingdom. The Make-Believe segments feature serialized, often week-long storylines that creatively mirror the episode''s overarching emotional theme, such as dealing with jealousy, welcoming a new sibling, or managing anger. The kingdom is ruled by the pompous but well-meaning King Friday XIII and his wife Queen Sara Saturday. Other prominent puppet residents include the timid Daniel Striped Tiger, the mischievous and eccentric Lady Elaine Fairchilde, and neighbors X the Owl and Henrietta Pussycat. Human characters, including Lady Aberlin, Handyman Negri, Chef Brockett, and Officer Clemmons, frequently interact with the puppets and help them solve problems or mediate conflicts.

At the conclusion of the Make-Believe segment, the Trolley returns to the television house. Rogers reflects on the events that occurred in the magical kingdom, using them to reinforce the day''s lessons and validate the feelings of his young viewers. He typically feeds his fish and cleans up any materials used earlier in the episode. To close the program, Rogers sings the concluding song, "It''s Such a Good Feeling," while changing back into his dress shoes and suit jacket. Before leaving the house, he delivers a recurring farewell message, reminding viewers that they make each day special just by being themselves, and that he likes them exactly as they are.', 'Ended', 'PBS', 'Won''t you be my neighbor?', 5.987, 119, '1968-02-19', '2001-09-07', 6.1762, 'en', 'Mister Rogers'' Neighborhood', 'https://www.misterrogers.org/', 871, 31, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/qhbeRYVg120cBmc9XvGxvk6EmJF.jpg', '/bvbOrvK0hQveMB6SmQoAwIuRToh.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:35:37Z', '2026-04-03T17:36:20Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('9ec4db23-2cbc-4508-bda8-ca2796bc7346', 'The Really Wild Show', 5344, NULL, 1986, 'The Really Wild Show was a long-running British television show about wildlife, broadcast by the BBC as part of their CBBC service to children. It also runs on Animal Planet in the US.

The show was broadcast continuously since 21 January 1986. In April 2006 the BBC announced that the show would be axed that summer, and as such the last ever episode was shown in April 2006, giving the show a run of 20 years.', 'The series operates as an educational wildlife and nature programme aimed at children, originally characterized by its vibrant, highly interactive studio environment. Hosted by a rotating cast of passionate wildlife experts—most notably including Terry Nutkins, Chris Packham, Michaela Strachan, Nick Baker, and Steve Backshall—the show''s primary mission is to make zoology, biology, and conservation accessible and exciting for a young audience. In its classic format, the series is anchored in a colorful studio filled with an audience of enthusiastic children, seamlessly blending live animal demonstrations with informative commentary.

During the studio segments, the hosts introduce a wide variety of exotic and domestic animals directly to the children. The presenters often bring the creatures into the seating area, allowing the young audience members to pet, hold, or observe them up close. These in-studio interactions serve as a springboard to educate viewers about the animals'' physical characteristics, diets, and unique biological adaptations. The hosts foster an energetic and informal atmosphere, frequently answering spontaneous questions from the studio audience, busting common animal myths, and reading letters or showing artwork sent in by viewers at home. 

Interspersed with the studio activities are pre-recorded field reports that take the presenters into varied environments, ranging from local British woodlands and coastlines to distant, exotic locations across the globe, such as the African savanna or the Amazon rainforest. These documentary-style inserts allow the show to feature large, dangerous, or elusive wildlife—such as lions, whales, polar bears, and crocodiles—that cannot be safely brought into a television studio. As the presenters track down these animals in their natural habitats, they provide detailed on-the-ground narration about the species'' ecological roles, mating rituals, and survival strategies. 

A prominent and recurring theme throughout the series is environmental conservation and animal welfare. The show frequently tackles serious ecological issues, highlighting the plight of endangered species and explaining the real-world impacts of habitat destruction, pollution, and poaching. The presenters regularly champion various wildlife campaigns, encouraging children to take an active, responsible interest in protecting the natural world and showing ways they can help local wildlife in their own gardens. Over its two-decade run, the format of the series gradually evolved; later seasons transitioned away from the traditional live studio audience in favor of a magazine-style format filmed entirely on location, though the program maintained its core educational focus and adventurous spirit until its conclusion.', 'Ended', 'BBC One', '', 7.0, 2, '1986-01-21', '2006-05-18', 1.3809, 'en', 'The Really Wild Show', 'https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/b0078px4', 217, 19, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/nM9ciPeh1UkcXXk9u0X4pmtypNi.jpg', '/nzO6OfgBpB24lbxkSJfI43MuBX2.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:21:14Z', '2026-04-03T17:21:46Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('a4832ebc-c150-49f9-966b-c33e86a06dfa', 'Fraggle Rock', 2957, 76085, 1983, 'The Fraggles are a fun-loving community of creatures who live in a subterranean fantasy land where they love to play, sing and dance their cares away, sharing their world with the tiny Doozers and the giant Gorgs. The series teaches empathy and tolerance and encourages children to understand people different from themselves.', 'The series follows the interconnected lives of several distinct species residing within and around a subterranean cavern system known as Fraggle Rock. The primary protagonists are the Fraggles, a race of small, colorful, furry creatures who live a mostly carefree existence centered around playing, singing, and exploring. The show focuses on a core group of five Fraggles: Gobo, the brave and practical leader; Mokey, the spiritual and artistic dreamer; Red, the energetic and competitive swimmer; Wembley, Gobo''s cheerful but perpetually indecisive best friend; and Boober, a pessimistic and superstitious Fraggle who prefers doing laundry to adventuring.

Within the caves, the Fraggles coexist with the Doozers, a society of tiny, green, construction-obsessed workers. The Doozers spend their lives building intricate crystalline towers and bridges out of a material derived from radishes. The Fraggles consider these constructions a delicious snack, and their constant eating of the Doozer architecture is actually vital to the ecosystem, as it clears space for the Doozers to continue building, establishing a necessary symbiotic relationship between the two species.

Beyond the caves lies the territory of the Gorgs, a family of massive, hairy, humanoid farmers consisting of Pa, Ma, and their bumbling son, Junior. The Gorgs believe themselves to be the rulers of the universe and consider the Fraggles to be crop-stealing pests. The Fraggles frequently brave the Gorgs'' garden to harvest radishes, which are required to sustain both the Fraggles and the Doozers. Near the Gorgs'' garden resides Marjory the Trash Heap, a sentient, magical compost pile who serves as a wise oracle for the Fraggles, assisted by her two rat-like companions, Philo and Gunge.

Fraggle Rock is also directly connected to the human world, which the Fraggles refer to as "Outer Space." This connection is a small hole in the wall of a workshop owned by Doc, an eccentric human inventor, and his loyal sheepdog, Sprocket. Sprocket is fully aware of the Fraggles'' existence and constantly tries to alert Doc, who remains entirely oblivious to the magical world just behind his baseboard. Gobo''s uncle, Traveling Matt, is a courageous explorer who ventures into Outer Space to study humans, whom he calls "Silly Creatures." Matt regularly sends postcards to Gobo detailing his humorous, often entirely incorrect deductions about human life, forcing Gobo to frequently sneak into Doc''s workshop and evade Sprocket to retrieve the mail from the wastebasket.

As the series progresses across its five seasons, the environmental and social interconnectedness of these different species becomes the central narrative focus. Conceived by creator Jim Henson as an allegory for international relations, the show uses the varying perspectives of the Fraggles, Doozers, Gorgs, and humans to teach children about empathy, prejudice, and conflict resolution, heavily incorporating original music to convey its themes. The series concludes with the various species finally breaking down the barriers between them. In the finale, the Gorgs give up their royal titles and Doc finally meets the Fraggles, discovering that the magic of Fraggle Rock exists everywhere before he and Sprocket move away to a new home.', 'Ended', 'HBO', '', 7.3, 185, '1983-01-10', '1987-03-30', 14.764, 'en', 'Fraggle Rock', 'https://www.henson.com/fraggle-rock/', 96, 5, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/nyvNej9aHC2wydFflPMrASS6MF7.jpg', '/fyUt0I4tSnclpg7WZJVRKHMSjcz.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:34:59Z', '2026-04-03T17:35:53Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('a62fb790-5c8f-4d7d-8c40-d76543b40070', 'Between the Lions', 4302, 74776, 2000, 'Between the Lions is a PBS Kids puppet television series designed to promote reading. The show is a co-production between WGBH in Boston and Sirius Thinking, Ltd., in New York City, in association with Mississippi Public Broadcasting, in Mississippi. The show has won seven Daytime Emmy awards between 2001 and 2007. The target audience is children 4 to 7 years old. It has the same puppet style as Sesame Street and several season 2 episodes, notably in Dance in Smarty Pants, had a few characters from Sesame Street guest appearing. Between The Lions started its 10th and final season on September 20, 2010. The Show Ended in November, 22 2010 Along with Reading Rainbow', 'The series focuses on a family of anthropomorphic lions working and living in the Barnaby B. Busterfield III Memorial Public Library, starring alongside characters such as Click, an electronic, anthropomorphic computer mouse; the Information Hen, who answers library calls; and Heath, a dinosaur who serves as the library''s thesaurus. The program''s format is intended to promote literacy and reading; in each episode, the lions introduce a picture book to the audience and read it. Some episodes have featured adaptations of well-known folktales, ancient myths, or fables, while others have featured popular storybooks, or shown the lions learning or benefiting from the lessons presented by the story.
The series often features an array of educational segments, including parodies of notable media redesigned educationally for younger audiences or simple animations. A sub-plot features a pair of pigeons named Walter and Clay, comically infuriating a living bust of the library''s deceased founder, Barnaby B. Busterfield III, located in the dome of the library, which is normally intended for comic relief and bizarre wordplay.
After the fourth season, the series underwent a noticeable format change. Notably, the show consisted of two 10-minute shorts, each a condensation of an earlier episode, tied together and united around a theme. The library set was greatly reduced, with just one small section as the primary location. Past segments such as "Magic Time," "Sam Spud", and "The Monkey Pop-Up Theater" were replaced with new animated ones such as "Joy Learno" and "The Trampolini Brothers". Later episodes shifted away from the earlier focus on reading, and stories were just told to tie into the theme of the episode. Major characters such as Busterfield, Walter and Clay, Heath, and Martha Reader vanished from the show as well, despite still appearing in the intro.', 'Ended', 'PBS', '', 7.5, 6, '2000-04-03', '2010-11-22', 6.8398, 'en', 'Between the Lions', 'http://www.pbskids.org/lions/', 140, 10, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/3MIfUYQvR58F99QFnSiwCo7fTnF.jpg', '/oAqPZZ4I2NPwZDRnwd2zZSAWmcr.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:12:27Z', '2026-04-03T17:12:35Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('abeeff5a-ac6a-44e0-910b-8bc866b1556d', 'The Muppet Show', 4459, 72476, 1976, 'Go behind the curtains as Kermit the Frog and his muppet friends struggle to put on a weekly variety show.', '"The Muppet Show Theme" (written by Henson and Sam Pottle in 1976) is the show''s theme song. While the opening sequence changed from season to season, the overall concept remained the same. Each episode began with "The Muppet Show" logo on a title card. The centre of the "O" then opened to reveal host Kermit the Frog, who announced, "It''s The Muppet Show, with our very special guest star, !" usually cheering afterwards.
During the first season, the theme song contained a joke from Fozzie Bear and featured Kermit introducing the guest star ("To introduce our guest star, that''s what I''m here to do, so it really makes me happy to introduce to you..."). At the song''s end, Gonzo the Great appeared in front of the "Muppet Show" banner, attempting to play the "O" in "Show" like a gong, with various comical results.
From the second to fourth seasons, the joke and Kermit''s introduction were replaced by a short quip from Statler and Waldorf, then a shot of the audience singing "Why don''t they get things started?" The fifth season version had an extra verse from the hecklers ("Why do we always come here? I guess we''ll never know. It''s like a kind of torture to have to watch the show!"). At the end of the song, Gonzo appeared inside the "O" in "Show" to play the final note on a trumpet; again, with various comical results.
Each episode ended with an extended instrumental performance of "The Muppet Show Theme" by the Muppet orchestra before Statler and Waldorf gave their last comment of the night, followed by Zoot playing an off-key final note on his saxophone. These last comment sequences would sometimes include other Muppets on the balcony.
Every season, the TV version of the song was presented with re-worked lyrics. While the opening sequence evolved visually over the show''s five seasons, the musical composition remained essentially the same. Over the years, the song has become a staple of the Muppets franchise as a whole.

The Muppet Theater is the setting for The Muppet Show, a grand old vaudeville house that has seen better days. In a 1976 episode guest starring Jim Nabors, Kermit identifies the name of the theatre as The Benny Vandergast Memorial Theater, although other episodes identify it as "the Muppet Theater". It is also identified as "Muppet Theater" in the 2002 television film It''s a Very Merry Muppet Christmas Movie. It is in this film that the theatre becomes registered as a historical landmark. In the film, the theatre is shown to be in New York City.
According to the 1991 picture book The Phantom of the Muppet Theater, the theatre was built by a stage actor named John Stone in 1802. An alternative exterior is also shown in the book.
Locations seen in the Muppet Theater include backstage right (which includes Kermit''s desk), the dressing rooms, the attic (seen in four compilation videos released in 1985), the canteen, the prop room, the stage, Statler and Waldorf''s box, the auditorium, reception, the recording studio, the stage door lobby, the boiler room and the back alley. Some of these sets were later re-used as the Happiness Hotel in the 1981 film The Great Muppet Caper. A replica of the theatre was once used as the setting for the Muppet*Vision 3D, an attraction formerly located at the Disney''s Hollywood Studios and Disney California Adventure theme parks.
Scooter''s uncle J.P. Grosse owns the theatre and rents it to the Muppets. In a deleted scene from It''s a Very Merry Muppet Christmas Movie, Kermit reveals that J.P. has died and left the theatre to the Muppets in his will. This would have taken place some time after 1996, as J.P. can be seen (and referred to as such by the head of the KMUP network) in season 1 of Muppets Tonight, the 1990s reworking of The Muppet Show.
In the 2011 film The Muppets, a badly deteriorated version of the Muppet Theater is located on Hollywood Boulevard as part of the fictional Muppet Studios in Los Angeles. The Muppets reunite in hopes of raising enough money to buy the theatre.

Main article: List of Muppets
Many of the characters who appeared on The Muppet Show have appeared in previous and subsequent Muppet productions.

No guest star ever appeared twice on The Muppet Show, although John Denver appeared both on the show and in two specials (John Denver and the Muppets: A Christmas Together and John Denver & the Muppets: Rocky Mountain Holiday), while Dudley Moore reappeared in the special The Muppets Go to the Movies. Additionally, several guest stars from the series had cameos in the first three Muppet theatrical films, and the season four guest Alan Arkin had a cameo in The Muppets. Originally, the producers had to call on their personal contacts to appeal to them to appear, especially considering that doing so required an overseas trip to Britain. However, the situation changed when the ballet dancer Rudolf Nureyev offered to appear; his performance on this unusual TV programme produced so much favourable publicity that the series became one of the most sought after for various celebrities to appear in.
Many episodes featured actors, such as Gilda Radner, Steve Martin, Harvey Korman, Sylvester Stallone, Julie Andrews, Carol Burnett, Liza Minnelli, Christopher Reeve, Raquel Welch, Joel Grey and Dom DeLuise; some had veteran performers like Ethel Merman, Bob Hope, Danny Kaye, Don Knotts, Liberace, Peter Ustinov, James Coburn, Lena Horne, Zero Mostel and Vincent Price, and others had well-known pop singers, including Elton John, Diana Ross, Harry Belafonte, Kenny Rogers, Linda Ronstadt, Alice Cooper, Paul Simon, Helen Reddy, Twiggy, Debbie Harry, Buddy Rich, and Leo Sayer. Sayer''s show used his hit song "The Show Must Go On" with slightly changed lyrics in the second verse, from "I wish I could tear down the walls of this theatre" to "I wish I could tear down the walls of this Muppet Theatre". Some guest stars, such as the Monty Python star John Cleese, co-wrote much of their own episodes. The second-to-last episode, in 1981, had the then-James Bond actor Roger Moore, while the final episode to be taped guest-starred the actor and dancer Gene Kelly. Mark Hamill appeared in one episode as both himself and Luke Skywalker, his role in the Star Wars film series. Two of Henson''s childhood idols, Edgar Bergen and Milton Berle, also guest-starred during the second season.
In 1977, Rita Moreno won the Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Individual Performance in a Variety or Music Program for her appearance. The next year, Peter Sellers — who chose not to appear as himself, instead appearing in a variety of characters — and Bernadette Peters earned nominations for the same award. One 1981 episode had the staff writer Chris Langham (who wrote some episodes of show, starting in the third season) guest-starring when Richard Pryor was unable to make the taping of the episode at the last minute.
An early tradition was to present the guest star with a Muppet likeness of themselves as a parting gift at the end of the show. This lasted for the first two episodes produced, with Connie Stevens and Juliet Prowse. The high cost and effort of creating these unique Muppets, scheduling conflicts, and potential legal issues contributed to the decline of this practice, although Muppet caricatures and parodies continued to appear. The practice did, however, take place for actors Michael Caine and Tim Curry, who were the lead performers in the films The Muppet Christmas Carol and Muppet Treasure Island, released in 1992 and 1996 respectively.

