^ "The 10 best Muppet Show guests". The Observer. 22 January 2012. Retrieved 30 March 2020.

^ "The Muppet Show". Emmys. Archived from the original on 8 October 2012. Retrieved 4 August 2012.', 'Ended', 'ITV1', 'It''s time to raise the curtain on The Muppet Show!', 8.009, 340, '1976-09-05', '1981-03-15', 15.6341, 'en', 'The Muppet Show', '', 120, 5, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/x1aZsosXqaCpZTI12lLpNYIj6HX.jpg', '/9UyPl2zR9dckpnmBWP6Xz4E6P3G.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:35:23Z', '2026-04-03T17:35:30Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('ac1cb94b-5f2d-421b-968d-f73ee9964dfa', 'The Wombles', 15122, 80104, 1973, 'The misadventures of a small community dedicated to cleaning up litter and putting it to their own use.', 'The series follows the daily lives of the Wombles, a community of secretive, furry, pointy-nosed creatures who live in a large, complex burrow situated beneath Wimbledon Common in London. The Wombles are fiercely dedicated to a single, environmentally conscious mission: cleaning up the litter left behind by humans and putting it to practical use. Operating under their famous motto, "Make good use of bad rubbish," the Wombles function as a highly organized and cooperative society. They spend their days venturing out onto the Common to scavenge for discarded items, bringing them back to their cozy underground home, which is entirely furnished and powered by repurposed human refuse. 

The burrow is inhabited by a distinct cast of characters, whose names are traditionally chosen from a large atlas. The community is led by the wise and stern patriarch, Great Uncle Bulgaria, who oversees operations from his study while wearing his signature tartan shawl and cap. The practical needs of the burrow are handled by Tobermory, a gruff but highly skilled engineer who acts as the community''s primary inventor, transforming everyday human garbage into ingenious tools, vehicles, and gadgets. The younger Wombles assigned to do the daily gathering include the energetic and somewhat bossy Bungo; the athletic, golf-loving, but dim-witted Tomsk; and the quiet, scientifically minded Wellington. Perhaps the most iconic resident is Orinoco, an endearingly lazy and perpetually hungry Womble who frequently tries to shirk his cleaning duties to take naps or sneak food, though he is capable of surprising bravery and effort when the situation demands it. The burrow is kept well-fed by Madame Cholet, a resourceful cook who prepares meals using natural ingredients foraged from the Common, such as bracken, fungi, and berries.

Episodes typically revolve around the Wombles'' daily excursions to collect rubbish, relying on a comforting and low-stakes narrative structure. A standard plotline involves the Wombles being sent out on a working shift on the Common, where they must navigate various mild obstacles, such as changing weather, encounters with local wildlife, or Orinoco''s attempts to avoid work. A major source of suspense is the strict rule that Wombles must remain completely unseen by humans. Much of the comedy and narrative drive stems from the Wombles discovering a seemingly mundane item discarded by humans—such as an umbrella, a discarded tire, or an old newspaper—and attempting to understand its original purpose before bringing it back for Tobermory to reengineer. The entire series is presented through stop-motion animation and is uniquely unified by the narration of Bernard Cribbins, who voices all the characters and provides the ambient storytelling, lending the show a gentle, storybook atmosphere.

Based on the children''s novels by Elisabeth Beresford, the series places a heavy emphasis on themes of environmentalism, conservation, and sustainability. Highly progressive for its time in the 1970s, the program educated its young audience on the importance of recycling and the negative impact of littering, framing these concepts not as a chore, but as a creative and exciting adventure. In addition to its ecological message, the series champions strong prosocial themes of teamwork, cross-generational respect, and the value of community labor, demonstrating how the diverse personalities of the Wombles work together to maintain a harmonious and self-sufficient home.', 'Ended', 'BBC One', '', 7.5, 14, '1973-02-05', '1975-10-24', 1.3772, 'en', 'The Wombles', '', 60, 2, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/xoxXtDpwJGBkoGiOCYdRI8wcxac.jpg', '/sLs1mTL2DPh0ySpvbN1v7I1YklT.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:23:19Z', '2026-04-03T17:23:51Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('ac75c382-1246-42cd-95b8-7e8ae011ffb6', 'The Magic School Bus', 7248, 71416, 1994, 'An eccentric schoolteacher takes her class on wondrous educational field trips with the help of a magical school bus.', 'The animated series follows the adventures of eccentric teacher Ms. Frizzle and her eight students as they explore the wonders of science on their exciting field trips. Their journey takes them to different locations, time periods, and encounters with various creatures, all while learning about science and the world around them. The school they attend, Walkerville Elementary, is set in the fictitious town of Walkerville. The characters travel all over the world and into space throughout the duration of the show.', 'Ended', 'PBS Kids', 'Seat belts, everyone!', 7.7, 126, '1994-09-10', '1997-12-06', 14.3285, 'en', 'The Magic School Bus', '', 52, 4, 0, 'Scripted', 'CA,US', '/3A70wxUpplD4V3IcL8WmNFBgAbV.jpg', '/pWZjTb2ixwVEzvnsvv3KYz5pvIs.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:37:47Z', '2026-04-03T17:37:54Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('afc53873-54e4-4581-a045-3b0871b9e185', 'Eyewitness', 88869, 256481, 1995, 'Dive into the field of natural science, Discover the Solar System or the various functions of the human body. The information is presented in the "Eyewitness Museum", a computer-generated science museum. Various exhibits are shown, and stock video footage is usually seen through large windows or other depressions in the wall.', 'Eyewitness is a documentary series. Each half-hour episode focuses on a single subject in the field of natural science, such as the Solar System or the various functions of the human body, similar in form to the book series on which it was based, with most being based, in part or in whole, off of existing book titles at the time, with few exceptions (though some titles, such as "Planets" and "Natural Disaster" started off as episodes and were made into books years later).', 'Ended', 'BBC Two', 'See the world as never before', 6.0, 1, '1995-06-05', '1997-11-24', 2.7736, 'en', 'Eyewitness', '', 40, 3, 0, 'Documentary', 'GB', '/514KMLuumHPQyPDD3IOSerubtLh.jpg', '/ibPvNTOPWSuzeFpzAhpuvZ7YYPT.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:39:03Z', '2026-04-03T17:39:09Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('b6b9d3ae-6273-46ed-9e99-538f94677c34', 'McGee and Me!', 4642, 72267, 1989, 'After moving to a new home in Indiana, eleven year old aspiring cartoonist Nick Martin and his animated friend, McGee, learn lessons relating to growing up and morality.', 'McGee and Me! stars Nicholas "Nick" Martin, an 11-year-old aspiring artist and cartoonist who lives with his family in the town of Eastfield, Indiana. His father, David, is a newspaper journalist while his mother, Elizabeth, works at a counseling center. Nick, his parents, his older sister Sarah, and their younger sister Jamie, all first move to Eastfield in the first episode, moving into their grandmother''s house to live with her. Nick often spends his time drawing and conversing with his imaginary friend, an animated cartoon character named McGee, who has wacky adventures of his own and serves as Nick''s constant companion and confidant. Nick''s life is constantly challenged by worldly trials and tribulations that put his morals and convictions to the test, with Nick learning valuable lessons along the way and, at times, facing the consequences of his actions.
His friends include Louis Armstrong, a streetwise kid and Nick''s best friend, Renee Johnson, a pretty girl and a good student who hangs out with Nick and Louis, Phillip Monroe, a young "nerdy" boy in Nick''s class who''s very smart and sometimes picked on by older kids, and, in the last two episodes, Jordan Michaels, an athletic football-playing student who''s also into computer graphics. In the first nine episodes, Nick''s main adversary is Derrick Cryder, a bully who constantly picks on Nick, Phillip, and other kids, but by the end of the Christmas episode, he reforms when he saves Nick from a gang member after Nick shows him kindness. Derrick later serves as a voice of reason for Nick in Episode 12 after Nick strains his friendship with his other friends.
The first nine episodes feature Nick in waning years of elementary school, while the final three episodes, titled The New Adventures of McGee and Me!, see Nick enter junior high school. The actors at the time were much older than their characters'' ages, giving Nick the appearance of a high schooler instead, due to the final three episodes having been produced a couple of years after the first nine.', 'Ended', 'Trinity Broadcasting Network', '', 7.3, 3, '1989-06-04', '1992-06-21', 2.4467, 'en', 'McGee and Me!', 'http://www.mcgeeandme.com/', 12, 2, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/pX4LUyTkAEZgRzGBMYvcn5dMgAG.jpg', '/fbydQkLcwe2ACzmHB0co0zcCIvv.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:40:08Z', '2026-04-03T17:40:13Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bcde3737-b03b-4a75-b676-ba4495ceb9d8', 'The Real Housewives of New York City', 14808, 84669, 2008, 'A fast-paced reality show that follows several incredibly busy and ambitious Manhattan women. Watch as they balance envious social calendars, challenging careers, and motherhood, with the hustle and bustle of the big city all around.', '*The Real Housewives of New York City* premiered on March 4, 2008, originally developed under the working title *Manhattan Moms* before being integrated into the larger *Real Housewives* franchise. The first season introduced an ensemble consisting of Bethenny Frankel, Luann de Lesseps, Alex McCord, Ramona Singer, and Jill Zarin. Early storylines focused on the women''s elite social standing, Frankel''s hustle to establish her natural foods brand, de Lesseps'' strict adherence to etiquette as a French countess, and McCord''s aggressive social climbing. The second season added Kelly Killoren Bensimon, while the third season introduced Sonja Morgan. The third season''s narrative was largely dominated by the bitter and spectacular collapse of Frankel and Zarin''s previously inseparable friendship. The season culminated in an infamous cast trip to the Virgin Islands, widely dubbed "Scary Island," which was marked by Bensimon''s erratic, highly publicized mental breakdown. Following the third season, Frankel departed the series to star in her own spin-offs.

The fourth season added Cindy Barshop but was defined by a deeply fractured cast dynamic that split the women into two opposing factions. Seeking to revamp the series, Bravo initiated a massive cast overhaul, firing Zarin, McCord, Bensimon, and Barshop ahead of the fifth season and replacing them with Aviva Drescher, Carole Radziwill, and Heather Thomson. The sixth season introduced Kristen Taekman and heavily centered on Drescher''s increasingly dramatic antics. This climaxed at the season finale party at Le Cirque, where Drescher, amidst a heated argument, infamously removed her prosthetic leg and slammed it onto the dining table. Following the incident, Drescher was let go from the series.

The seventh season fundamentally shifted the show''s dynamic with the highly publicized return of Frankel, alongside the introduction of Dorinda Medley. The eighth season added Julianne "Jules" Wainstein and documented de Lesseps'' whirlwind romance with businessman Tom D''Agostino. The relationship became a massive point of contention when Frankel presented de Lesseps with photographic proof of D''Agostino''s infidelity. Despite the revelation, de Lesseps proceeded to marry him in the ninth season—which also introduced Tinsley Mortimer—only to file for divorce seven months later. The tenth season was heavily defined by de Lesseps'' shocking arrest in Palm Beach for battery on a police officer and her subsequent journey through rehabilitation. Simultaneously, the season chronicled the deeply acrimonious deterioration of Frankel and Radziwill''s once-unbreakable friendship, leading to Radziwill''s permanent departure. Following the eleventh season, which captured Frankel grappling with the sudden death of her on-and-off boyfriend Dennis Shields, Frankel departed the series for a second time.

The twelfth season introduced streetwear brand founder Leah McSweeney, while Mortimer departed midway through filming to relocate to Chicago. The season saw mounting friction over Medley''s increasingly hostile, alcohol-fueled outbursts, which ultimately led to her dismissal. The thirteenth season introduced attorney and broadcaster Eboni K. Williams, making history as the franchise''s first Black housewife. Filmed at the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, the season''s narrative was heavily dominated by intense, polarizing conversations regarding race, politics, and a glaring generational divide among the cast. The deeply fractured dynamics and a sharp decline in ratings resulted in Bravo breaking tradition by canceling the reunion special and placing the series on an indefinite hiatus.

In a franchise first, Bravo completely rebooted the series for its fourteenth season, dismissing the entire existing cast in favor of a new, younger, and more diverse ensemble of women. The revamped cast featured content creator Sai De Silva, model Ubah Hassan, real estate agent Erin Lichy, former J.Crew president Jenna Lyons, fashion publicist Jessel Taank, and marketing consultant Brynn Whitfield. Storylines in the rebooted era focused on the women navigating the overlapping, highly competitive spheres of New York''s fashion and real estate industries. The fourteenth season centered on petty interpersonal feuds, Taank''s intimacy struggles with her husband following the birth of their twins, and the emotional revelations of De Silva and Whitfield''s traumatic childhoods. The reboot continued into its fifteenth season, adding art curator Racquel Chevremont as a main cast member and introducing fashion designer Rebecca Minkoff in a "friend of" capacity, with the narrative focusing on shifting alliances and escalating tensions over the women''s professional and personal loyalties.', 'Returning Series', 'Bravo', 'New York City is my playground.', 5.7, 65, '2008-03-04', '2025-02-04', 13.2938, 'en', 'The Real Housewives of New York City', 'http://www.bravotv.com/the-real-housewives-of-new-york-city', 292, 16, 1, 'Reality', 'US', '/dzy5nUc4MfTdQFBdbUFAT1FRPLQ.jpg', '/cowrSDpkCkNcmg5fO7gstaZLR1B.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:35:21Z', '2026-04-03T16:55:54Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('c2e22550-4dca-4da9-b833-e28e45132e0b', 'The Real Housewives of New Jersey', 18204, 89831, 2009, 'We head to the Garden State for this installment of the “Housewives” franchise, focusing on affluent Jersey girls who are more than just friends. The cast has changed through the seasons but the featured housewives through the years have included Caroline Manzo, a devoted wife and mother of three adult children; former Las Vegas cosmetologist Jacqueline Laurita, who is now a full-time housewife; and Teresa Giudice, a mother of four who runs her household without help from a nanny or personal assistant.', '*The Real Housewives of New Jersey* premiered on May 12, 2009, standing out from other installments in the franchise by heavily featuring a cast connected through family ties. The first season''s ensemble consisted of Caroline Manzo, her sister Dina Manzo, their sister-in-law Jacqueline Laurita, Teresa Giudice, and Danielle Staub. Early storylines centered on the women''s affluent lifestyles and tight-knit family dynamics, but the season''s narrative was ultimately dominated by the revelation of Staub''s criminal past via the book *Cop Without a Badge*, culminating in an explosive finale where Giudice infamously flipped a table during a dinner argument. The second season continued to focus on the severe isolation of Staub from the rest of the group, prompting Dina Manzo''s early departure and climaxing with a highly publicized chase at the Posche fashion show. Following the second season, Staub was let go from the series.

The third season fundamentally shifted the show''s dynamic with the additions of Giudice''s sister-in-law Melissa Gorga and cousin Kathy Wakile. The season immediately plunged into an intense, years-long family feud, beginning with a massive brawl between Giudice''s husband Joe and Gorga''s husband Joe at a family christening. The toxic family rivalry defined the series for several seasons, exacerbated by rumors of infidelity, financial disputes, and an infamous incident in the fourth season known as "Strippergate," where Giudice was accused of conspiring to expose Gorga''s alleged past as an exotic dancer. The fifth season attempted to mend the fractured family through a healing retreat in Lake George, though it resulted in a bitter physical altercation between the two Joes. Following the fifth season, Caroline Manzo departed the series to star in her own spin-off.

The sixth season saw the brief return of Dina Manzo, alongside new cast members Amber Marchese and identical twins Teresa Aprea and Nicole Napolitano. The season heavily chronicled the mounting legal struggles of Giudice and her husband, who both pleaded guilty to multiple federal fraud charges. Following a hiatus to accommodate Giudice''s 11-month prison sentence, the seventh season premiered with Giudice returning home, accompanied by the additions of philanthropist Siggy Flicker and veteran "friend of" Dolores Catania. Laurita, who had returned as a main cast member, found her long-standing friendships with Giudice and Gorga completely fractured, leading to her permanent departure at the end of the season.

The eighth season introduced lifestyle entrepreneur Margaret Josephs, who quickly engaged in a bitter, season-long feud with Flicker, prompting Flicker''s exit. The ninth season ushered in a new era with the additions of Jennifer Aydin and Jackie Goldschneider, while the narrative focused on the legal deportation of Giudice''s husband to Italy and the emotional toll on their four daughters. Seasons ten through twelve featured heightened interpersonal drama, including the brief return of Staub—whose involvement ended following a violent hair-pulling altercation with Josephs—and explosive rumors surrounding the cast''s marriages. Giudice sparked massive conflict by repeating infidelity rumors about Goldschneider''s husband, while Josephs exposed a past affair by Aydin''s husband, fundamentally fracturing the group''s dynamics. During this period, Giudice finalized her divorce and began a highly publicized relationship with businessman Luis "Louie" Ruelas.

The thirteenth season introduced Danielle Cabral and Rachel Fuda, while Goldschneider transitioned to a "friend" role alongside newcomer Jennifer Fessler. The season captured the absolute collapse of Giudice and Gorga''s already fragile relationship, fueled by allegations surrounding Ruelas and a contentious pizza business deal. The family warfare culminated in Gorga and her husband refusing to attend Giudice and Ruelas''s extravagant wedding. The deeply entrenched divide carried over into the fourteenth season, which saw the cast firmly split into two opposing factions that largely refused to interact off-camera. The season was marked by shifting alliances, including Goldschneider unexpectedly befriending Giudice, and a physical altercation between Aydin and Cabral that led to their temporary suspension from filming. Due to the completely fractured and toxic nature of the group, Bravo broke tradition by canceling the traditional reunion special, instead concluding the season with an explosive and unresolved final sit-down at a local restaurant.', 'Returning Series', 'Bravo', 'You’ll never see how toxic someone is until you breathe fresher air.', 6.0, 63, '2009-05-12', '2024-08-11', 7.0479, 'en', 'The Real Housewives of New Jersey', 'http://www.bravotv.com/the-real-housewives-of-new-jersey', 252, 14, 1, 'Reality', 'US', '/n3ZNgp7IpQOUXoGoV8svZccZeoP.jpg', '/12Xbio78KHTn5qO94XlE6s8gtQb.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:35:14Z', '2026-04-03T16:55:16Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('c93ccfa0-2b50-4b25-a25c-c6f531496e68', 'The Demon Headmaster', 19094, 72455, 1996, 'The Demon Headmaster is a British television series based on the children''s books by Gillian Cross of the same name. Made for CBBC, the drama was first broadcast between 1996 and 1998. The first series contained six episodes, and aired twice weekly from 2 to 18 January 1996, the second series contained seven episodes, and aired once a week from 25 September to 6 November 1996, and the third series contained six episodes, and aired twice weekly from 6 to 22 January 1998.

School location scenes in the first series were filmed at Hatch End High School, in Hatch End, Harrow, North West London and The Royal Masonic School for Girls in Rickmansworth, Hertfordshire. Other scenes were filmed around West London and the Vulcan Tower is in fact the Atrium building in Uxbridge. CGI was used to make this building appear on a traffic island close to Warwick Avenue tube station. Some scenes in the later series were filmed in the village of Sarratt, Hertfordshire and other locations in Hertfordshire and Buckinghamshire.', 'Note: Also sometimes known as Look Into My Eyes, though this title does not appear on the screen.
Dinah Glass moves in with the Hunter family and starts going to the same school as her foster brothers Lloyd and Harvey. It is not easy, as she finds the school strange, and her foster brothers seem to hate her. Pupils suddenly talk like robots and do unusual things, and even Dinah finds herself acting oddly. She is sure the headmaster has some kind of power over them, and is determined to find out more. But the Demon Headmaster is equally determined to stop her.

Octopus Dare is the new computer game at school – everyone is playing it, but only Dinah is any good at it. Soon, all she can think about is Octopus Dare. She wins a place at the grand final, and is delighted. But there is something strange about it all that scares Dinah, so her friends come with her to the final. Before long they find themselves in trouble, as the Computer Director turns out to be their old enemy, the Demon Headmaster.

Dinah''s father is headhunted for a new job at the Biogenetic Research Centre, but the Demon Headmaster is the director. This time, his lust for power sees him meddling with evolution itself; the Headmaster has created an Evolution Accelerator, for which he wants Dinah''s DNA to create a "perfect" human, with Dinah''s intellect but no emotions. He then puts Dinah into terrible danger to test his theories.

On Christmas Day 1997, CBBC showed The Demon Headmaster Takes Over TV, a recorded version of the CBBC Pantomime, performed at the CBBC Big Bash in the National Exhibition Centre, Birmingham that year. Terrence Hardiman did not appear live during the show, appearing only as pre-recorded images on a video wall. Whilst the first two series have been repeated several times on television, this special was only shown once and has never been repeated. or made available as a DVD extra.

Dinah and her brothers are pretty sure they are rid of the Demon Headmaster once and for all. When the army starts dismantling the biogenetic research centre, Dinah contacts Professor Claudia Rowe, who is an expert on biology and genetics at the nearby university. With Dinah''s help, she saves some of the precious materials and they become friends. Then strange things start to happen.
Libraries start closing and their books are taken away. Telephones stop working, and there is no internet access. People start talking like robots, even Claudia Rowe, and more and more of them seem to be wearing strange badges. It all adds up to one thing: the Demon Headmaster must be back. Dinah''s search leads her to the university where she finds he has taken over the artificial intelligence project and is developing a Hyperbrain, a computer with superhuman intelligence and the potential to control all information in the world. Both the Demon Headmaster and the newly-sentient Hyperbrain want Dinah for knowledge she possesses.

^ "Children''s BBC". BBC Genome. 25 December 1997. Archived from the original on 26 February 2017. Retrieved 25 February 2017.', 'Ended', 'CBBC', '', 6.7, 9, '1996-01-02', '1998-01-22', 3.1108, 'en', 'The Demon Headmaster', 'http://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/b0084rrc', 19, 3, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/p1PvMAsXgonghSV8RmXP6VRLpIX.jpg', NULL, '2026-04-03T17:40:34Z', '2026-04-03T17:40:40Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('caaa394a-7503-4a13-b3b9-a2f97aa40065', 'The Berenstain Bears', 4665, 72502, 2003, 'We follow a family of bears, known as the Berenstain Bears, as they figure out life together. With friendly neighbors and close friends, the journey is never boring. Inspired by the book series written by Stan and Jan Berenstain.', 'The series is set in a forested land populated only by anthropomorphic bears and primarily focuses on the title Berenstain Bears. The Berenstain Bears are a family residing in the rural community of Bear Country. The family consisted of Mama Bear, Papa Q. Bear, Brother Bear, and Sister Bear.
Although numerous episodes are based on the books and promote the same morals as encouraged in the picture books from which their plots originated, the program''s faithfulness to the original series is slightly mixed on account of a number of later episodes following original storylines.
Nonetheless, they mostly portray the same circumstance depicted in the original Berenstain Bears storybooks quite accurately and concentrate on the messages and lessons learned by the family through their different experiences, such as generosity and responsibility, as well as the daily lives of the bears.', 'Ended', 'Treehouse TV', '', 6.6, 18, '2003-01-06', '2004-09-28', 4.5069, 'en', 'The Berenstain Bears', 'http://www.berenstainbears.com', 80, 3, 0, 'Scripted', 'CA', '/cRA1KVomv7d9avL7UpdPBJmASrv.jpg', '/8Ehogxv09PYKQkqVibwS4mcpg5C.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:40:21Z', '2026-04-03T17:40:27Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('cd02dceb-3b22-404f-9b72-75e218875e6d', 'Reading Rainbow', 10048, 77547, 1983, 'Journey to exciting places and build a lasting connection with your favorite books. Each episode centers on a theme from a book, or other children''s literature, which is explored through a number of segments or stories.', 'Each episode of the series is hosted by LeVar Burton, who guides viewers through a variety of real-world locations to explore a specific theme, such as music, animals, history, or science. Early in the episode, Burton introduces the central concept, which is directly tied to the episode''s featured children''s book. The centerpiece of the broadcast is the narration of this featured book. Rather than fully animating the story, the program displays the book''s original printed illustrations, utilizing camera pans and zooms accompanied by background music and sound effects. The story is read aloud, frequently by a celebrity guest narrator.

Following or interspersed with the storybook reading, Burton embarks on various "field trips" to investigate the episode''s theme in greater depth. These documentary-style segments feature Burton interviewing experts, visiting manufacturing plants to see how everyday items are made, participating in cultural events, or interacting with nature. These excursions serve to bridge the gap between literature and real life, allowing the viewing audience to see the concepts from the featured book applied in the real world.

A recurring segment occurs near the end of every episode, which Burton introduces with his signature catchphrase: "But you don''t have to take my word for it." This segment features brief video reviews from school-aged children who give their own personal summaries and recommendations of three other books related to the episode''s theme. At the conclusion of the broadcast, Burton offers final thoughts on the day''s subject, typically encouraging viewers to visit their local library to check out the featured books, before signing off.', 'Ended', 'PBS', 'Take a look, it’s in a book, a Reading Rainbow!', 8.4, 45, '1983-06-06', '2006-11-10', 20.6321, 'en', 'Reading Rainbow', '', 155, 21, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/ndlSMn2zCP6MNIp6OYexGAa1Yj1.jpg', '/9kNBDpilniJFSOM0ciq12XUduRF.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:36:14Z', '2026-04-03T17:36:42Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('d0c42b0d-af04-4535-8b6e-c153528a949b', 'Bill Nye the Science Guy', 3483, 78494, 1993, 'It''s "Mr. Wizard" for a different decade. Bill Nye is the Science Guy, a host who''s hooked on experimenting and explaining. Picking one topic per show (like the human heart or electricity), Nye gets creative with teaching kids and adults alike the nuances of science.', 'The series is set in "Nye Labs," a highly stylized, gadget-filled laboratory where scientist and comedian Bill Nye, clad in his signature powder-blue lab coat and bow tie, serves as the energetic host. Designed to make science entertaining and accessible for a primary audience of pre-teens, the show utilizes a fast-paced, kinetic visual style—heavily influenced by MTV-era editing, dynamic camera angles, and quirky sound effects—to maintain high viewer engagement. Each half-hour episode focuses on a single scientific discipline or phenomenon, such as gravity, electricity, the human heart, or biodiversity. Through a blend of live-action demonstrations, sketch comedy, and on-location field footage, Nye explains complex scientific principles in a clear, highly visual manner, frequently emphasizing his signature catchphrase, "Science rules!"

Episodes are structurally built around a series of rapid-fire, recurring segments that break down the episode''s core theme into digestible lessons. In "Consider the Following," Nye directly addresses the audience to explain a specific aspect of the topic, typically accompanied by a hands-on laboratory demonstration or large-scale visual aid. The program encourages interactive learning through segments like "Nifty Home Experiment" (sometimes alternating with "Try This"), which guides viewers through simple, safe scientific experiments they can recreate using everyday household items. To ground the scientific concepts in real-world applications, the "Way Cool Scientist" segment profiles actual working professionals, researchers, and experts whose careers relate to the episode''s subject. Other frequent interstitial segments include "Did You Know That...", which provides rapid-fire trivia, and "Check It Out," featuring kids exploring scientific concepts in the field.

The narrative is heavily driven by sketch comedy and parodies of popular culture, television shows, and commercials. An unseen, enthusiastically booming announcer, voiced by Pat Cashman, frequently interacts with Nye, narrates various segments, and contributes to the show''s irreverent tone. The series also occasionally features celebrity guest appearances to help illustrate concepts, with actors, athletes, and musicians participating in brief comedic sketches. A hallmark of the series is its musical conclusion; nearly every episode culminates with the "Soundtrack of Science," a custom-produced music video that parodies a contemporary hit song or popular music genre, such as alternative rock, rap, or grunge. The lyrics are cleverly rewritten to summarize the scientific facts taught during the episode, often performed by Nye or guest musicians in elaborate costumes.

At the end of the episode, Nye typically offers a final, humorous wrap-up thought before making a swift, comedic exit from the frame. The broadcast concludes with the show''s iconic theme song—featuring the energetic, recurring chant of "Bill! Bill! Bill! Bill!"—playing over the closing credits. Alongside the credits, the program traditionally rolls a blooper reel featuring outtakes, flubbed lines, and behind-the-scenes antics from Nye and the production crew, reinforcing the show''s lighthearted, approachable take on science education.', 'Ended', 'PBS', '', 7.276, 87, '1993-09-10', '1998-06-20', 8.178, 'en', 'Bill Nye the Science Guy', 'https://billnye.com/', 100, 5, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/xdGGiQ8gBEwjyafLxW34IK8sblZ.jpg', '/7BG0EAfPn037Sj9h1cAB2srqQSX.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:37:20Z', '2026-04-03T17:37:47Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('d23bf5f8-54c2-468a-94f0-0aa0e8d7d130', 'Wishbone', 2152, 76132, 1995, 'Wishbone is a children''s television show. The show''s title character is a Jack Russell Terrier of the same name. Wishbone lives with his owner Joe Talbot in the fictional modern town of Oakdale, Texas. He daydreams about being the lead character of stories from classic literature He was known as "the little dog with a big imagination". Only the viewers and the characters in his daydreams can hear Wishbone speak. The characters from his daydreams see Wishbone as whatever famous character he is currently portraying and not as a dog.', 'A standard episode of Wishbone consists of an opening scene, introducing a contemporary plot in Wishbone''s hometown of Oakdale. Something about the situation reminds Wishbone of a famous work of literature, which he introduces to the viewer. The episode then cuts between an adaptation of that work, usually with Wishbone portraying the main character, and the contemporary plot. Occasionally, Wishbone will play a secondary character if the lead role is female (in Joan of Arc, he plays Louis de Conte) or difficult to relate to (he plays Sancho Panza in Don Quixote). The development of the contemporary plot parallels that of the literary work, particularly in their dénouements.
The last two minutes of nearly every episode are a behind-the-scenes featurette titled "Tail Ends", narrated by Wishbone and focusing on a production department whose work was particularly prominent in the episode, such as lighting for "The Legend of Sleepy Hollow" or make-up for Frankenstein.', 'Ended', 'PBS Kids', '', 8.0, 35, '1995-09-10', '1997-03-12', 5.1747, 'en', 'Wishbone', '', 50, 2, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/1cheNK4CBKopFScmgKP7bYjV3xs.jpg', '/pvnqWAu0b7wZtSq0hFqT3cWOkhg.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:10:41Z', '2026-04-03T17:10:47Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('d5b5f782-856a-48cf-a871-1f6a08cc4b6e', 'Children of the New Forest', 225746, 432482, 1998, 'A colonel during the 1640s English Civil War gets killed, leaving his four children in the care of their aunt. For fear of their safety the children were sent to a forester''s home in the forest. While there they encounter numerous adventures, including assisting King Charles who has also escaped from captivity.', 'Set in England during the English Civil War in the 1640s, the series follows the four aristocratic Beverley children—Edward, Humphrey, Alice, and Edith. After their Royalist father, Colonel Beverley, is killed in battle, the children are initially left in the care of their aunt. However, as Parliamentarian forces advance and burn down their ancestral home of Arnwood, the children''s lives are placed in grave danger. For their safety, they are secretly relocated to the isolated New Forest and placed under the protection of a loyal forester, Jacob Armitage. To avoid detection by the Roundheads, the siblings are forced to shed their noble identities, masquerade as Jacob''s peasant grandchildren, and quickly adapt to a physically demanding, rustic way of life.

As they adjust to their new environment, the children learn essential survival skills such as hunting, farming, and foraging. When Jacob eventually passes away, the siblings are left entirely to fend for themselves, with the eldest brother, Edward, taking on the heavy responsibility of leading the family. Their secluded existence in the forest is frequently interrupted by the ongoing war and a series of perilous adventures. Their loyalty to the Crown is ultimately put to the test when they encounter King Charles, who has escaped from Parliamentarian captivity and is seeking refuge. The children risk their own lives to assist the fugitive king, drawing them directly into the treacherous political conflict from which they had been hidden.

Throughout their time in the wilderness, Edward struggles with his aristocratic pride and a burning desire to actively fight for the Royalist cause. His rigid worldview is complicated when he crosses paths with Ephraim Heatherstone, a sympathetic Parliamentarian intendant appointed to oversee the New Forest, and his daughter, Patience. Despite being on opposing sides of the national conflict, Edward forms a deep bond with the Heatherstones. The siblings are forced to navigate shifting allegiances, local treachery, and the constant threat of discovery, ultimately relying on the resilience and practical skills they forged in the forest to survive the war and secure their family''s future.', 'Ended', NULL, '', 0.0, 0, '1998-11-15', '1998-12-20', 1.5085, 'en', 'Children of the New Forest', '', 6, 1, 0, 'Scripted', 'GB', '/33bjEWArruBwQfhYcnUlSMgL1ai.jpg', NULL, '2026-04-03T17:17:57Z', '2026-04-03T17:18:28Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('dae96dfb-8dbc-4f58-afec-0a36e3a371b1', 'Seinfeld', 1400, 79169, 1989, 'A stand-up comedian and his three offbeat friends weather the pitfalls and payoffs of life in New York City in the ''90s. It''s a show about nothing.', 'Many Seinfeld episodes are based on the writers'' real-life experiences, with the experiences reinterpreted for the characters'' storylines. For example, George''s storyline in "The Revenge" is based on Larry David''s experience at Saturday Night Live. "The Contest" is also based on David''s experiences. "The Smelly Car" storyline is based on Peter Mehlman''s lawyer friend, who could not get a bad smell out of his car. "The Strike" is based on Dan O''Keefe''s dad, who made up his own holiday: Festivus. Other stories take a variety of turns. "The Chinese Restaurant" consists of George, Jerry, and Elaine waiting for a table throughout the entire episode. "The Boyfriend", revolving around Keith Hernandez, extends through two episodes. "The Betrayal" is famous for using reverse chronology and was inspired by a similar plot device in a Harold Pinter play, Betrayal. Some stories were inspired by headlines and rumors, as explained in the DVD features "Notes About Nothing", "Inside Look" and "Audio Commentary". In "The Maestro", Kramer''s lawsuit is roughly similar to the McDonald''s coffee case. "The Outing" is based primarily on rumors that Larry Charles heard about Jerry Seinfeld''s sexuality.

The series was often described as "a show about nothing". However, in 2014, Seinfeld stated: "The pitch for the show, the real pitch, when Larry and I went to NBC in 1988, was we want to show how a comedian gets his material. The show "about nothing" was just a joke in an episode many years later, and Larry and I to this day are surprised that it caught on as a way that people describe the show because, to us, it''s the opposite of that." David similarly commented: "I like taking the worst qualities that a person has and trying to make something funny out of it. Doesn''t everybody do terrible things and have terrible thoughts? Just by trying to be as funny, you''re going to deal with a lot of things that are real, so the show''s really about something. The whole thing about the show being about nothing is ridiculous."
Much of the show''s humor is based upon repeated use of irony, incongruity, and (oftentimes unfortunate) coincidences. Additionally, guest characters are frequently introduced with little to no context, with a humorous focus on the atypical names of these characters, which often contain alliteration. In keeping with Seinfeld''s reputation as a clean comedian, though the show frequently contains dialogue around sexual themes, the show notably avoids using almost all explicit sexual terminology. Notably, in the popular episode "The Contest", whose plot line concerns a contest amongst the main characters to see which one can go the longest without masturbating, the word ''masturbation'' is never mentioned. Seinfeld broke several conventions of mainstream television. David is credited with refusing to follow a predictable sitcom formula that would have a romantic relationship develop between Jerry and Elaine.
The show offers no growth or reconciliation to its characters and eschews sentimentality. An episode is typically driven by humor interspersed with the superficial conflicts of characters with peculiar dispositions. Many episodes revolve around the characters'' involvement in the lives of others, with typically disastrous results. On the set, the notion that the characters should not develop or improve throughout the series was expressed as the "no hugging, no learning" rule. Larry David was adamant from the beginning that he did not want the characters to mature, grow or learn from their past mistakes. The characters are "thirty-something singles with vague identities, no roots, and conscious indifference to morals." Also unlike most sitcoms, there are no moments of pathos; the audience is never made to feel sorry for any of the characters. Even Susan''s death in "The Invitations" elicits no genuine emotions from anybody in the show. Seinfeld does not shy away from making light of tough topics, from death to illness to disability.
The show frequently engages in fourth-wall-breaking humor and self-satire. One such example is the story arc, where the characters promote a TV sitcom series named Jerry. The show within a show, Jerry was much like Seinfeld in that it was "about nothing," and Seinfeld played himself. The fictional Jerry was launched in the Season 4 finale, but unlike Seinfeld, it was not picked up as a series. Jerry is one of many examples of metafiction in the show. There are no fewer than 22 fictional movies featured, like Rochelle, Rochelle. Because of these several elements, Seinfeld became the first TV series since Monty Python''s Flying Circus to be widely described as postmodern.
Seinfeld is an avid Abbott and Costello fan and has cited The Abbott and Costello Show as an influence on Seinfeld: "Everybody on the show knows I''m a fan. We''re always joking about how we do stuff from their show. George and I will often get into a riff that has the rhythm from the old Abbott and Costello shows. And sometimes, I''ll hit George in the chest the way Abbott would hit Costello". The series includes numerous references to the team. George Costanza''s middle name is "Louis", after Costello. "The Old Man" episode features a cantankerous character named "Sid Fields" as a tribute to the landlord on the team''s TV show. Kramer''s friend is named Mickey Abbott. A copywriter for the J. Peterman catalog is named Eddie Sherman, after the team''s longtime agent. In Episode 30, Kramer hears the famous Abbott and Costello line, "His father was a mudder. His mother was a mudder."

Many terms were coined, popularized, or re-popularized in the series'' run and have become part of popular culture, including "Yada, yada, yada", "No soup for you!", "Master of my domain", "That''s a shame", and "Not that there''s anything wrong with that." The lexicon of Seinfeldian code words and recurring phrases that evolved around particular episodes is referred to as Seinlanguage, which is also the title of Jerry Seinfeld''s best-selling book on humor. These terms include "man hands", "shrinkage", "regift", and "double dip". 

A recurring feature of Seinfeld was its inclusion of specific products, especially candy, as plot points. These might be a central feature of a plot (e.g., Junior Mints, Twix, Chuckles, Jujyfruits, bite-size Three Musketeers, Snickers, Chunky, Oh Henry!, Drake''s Coffee Cake and PEZ), or an association of candy with a guest character (e.g. Oh Henry! bars) or simply a conversational aside (e.g., Chuckles, Clark Bar, Twinkies). A large number of non-candy products were also featured throughout the series.
The show''s creators claim that they weren''t engaging in a product placement strategy for commercial gain. One motivation for the use of real-world products, entirely unrelated to commercial considerations, is the comedy value of funny-sounding phrases and words. "I knew I wanted Kramer to think of watching the operation like going to see a movie," explained Seinfeld writer/producer Andy Robin in an interview published in The Hollywood Reporter. "At first, I thought maybe a piece of popcorn falls into the patient. I ran that by my brother, and he said, ''No, Junior Mints are just funnier.''"
Many advertisers capitalized on the popularity of Seinfeld. American Express created a webisode where Jerry Seinfeld and an animated Superman (voiced by Patrick Warburton, who played the role of Puddy) starred in its commercial. The makers of the Today Sponge created the "Spongeworthy" game on their website, inspired by "The Sponge". An advertisement featured Jason Alexander in a Chrysler commercial. In this, Alexander acts much like his character George, and his relationship with Lee Iacocca plays on George''s relationship with Steinbrenner. Similarly, Michael Richards was the focus of a series of advertisements for Vodafone, which ran in Australia, where he dressed and acted precisely like Kramer, including the trademark bumbling pratfalls. In addition, the show occasionally incorporated fictional products like a Scotch brand called "Hennigan''s" (a blend of "Hennessy" and "Brannigans") and a canned meat product called "Beef-a-reeno" (a parody of "Beef-a-roni").

The Seinfeld music was composed by Jonathan Wolff, who had previously composed for sitcoms including Who''s the Boss? and Married with Children. The pilot episode had a theme composed by a different composer, but Seinfeld felt it interfered with his standup sequences. Whereas previous sitcoms had been melodic and lyrical, Wolff did not want to create a typical theme and instead wanted to create an instantly recognizable "audio signature". He composed a theme comprising samples of slap bass taken from multiple sources, edited and processed with compression, equalized and other effects. He added percussion with finger snaps and mouth noises to create an organic "percolating energy" and blend with the "quirky" patterns of Seinfeld''s speech. When the network asked for a more conventional theme for the second season, David refused. Wolff composed variations on the theme to match the rhythms and dialogue of each episode.
In "The Note", the first episode of Season 3, the bumper music featured scatting female backup singers who sang a phrase that sounded like the tune "Easy to Beat". Jerry Seinfeld and executive producer Larry David both liked Wolff''s additions, and three episodes were produced with this new style of music. However, they had neglected to inform NBC and Castle Rock executives of the change, and when the season premiere aired, the executives were surprised and unimpressed and requested that they return to the original style. The subsequent two episodes were redone, leaving this episode as the only one with additional music elements. In the commentary of "The Note", Louis-Dreyfus facetiously suggests it was removed because the perceived lyric related closely to the low ratings at the time. The soundtrack was given a digital release on July 2, 2021.

