^ Cordero, Rosy (August 17, 2021). "''Real Housewives Of Dallas'': "No Plans" For Season 6 Return In 2022". Deadline. Retrieved June 6, 2024.', 'Canceled', 'Bravo', 'Dallas girls are sugar and spice, but I''m still working on nice.', 6.2, 17, '2016-04-11', '2021-05-11', 2.8227, 'en', 'The Real Housewives of Dallas', 'http://www.bravotv.com/the-real-housewives-of-dallas', 78, 5, 0, 'Reality', 'US', '/9QDd7EZgr5cWfyEirSYxVBIjDkS.jpg', '/2LEWLQ61ERXRffIhFrWfOxFFyil.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:35:03Z', '2026-04-03T16:35:06Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('e5b7531b-ff24-4990-9ee2-f96ec4789a5b', 'Five Children and It', 39977, 103701, 1991, 'The Psammead is an ''it'', an ancient, ill-tempered sand-fairy with a spider-shaped body, bat-like ears, and snail-like eyes. It is grumpy but has the power to grant one wish a day, which must be made before sunset. Five siblings—Cyril, Anthea, Robert, Jane, and infant Hilary—encounter It and experience the highs and lows of frivolous wish-making.', 'Five Children and It is a six-part TV mini-series that follows the wild summer adventures of five siblings—Robert, Anthea, Cyril, Jane, and their little brother, nicknamed "the Lamb." They''re staying at their uncle''s countryside house for the holidays when they stumble across something odd while poking around a nearby gravel pit: a Psammead, or Sand Fairy. Psammead can grant wishes, though it''s not exactly thrilled about it. The deal? One wish a day, but each one fades by sunset and tends to backfire in the funniest, most unexpected ways.
The kids, buzzing with excitement, dive right in, wishing for all sorts of things: piles of gold, wings sprouting from their backs, epic adventures, or even trips to far-off times and places. But their big ideas, combined with the Psammead''s habit of taking their words way too literally, quickly turn into a mess of hilarious misadventures. For instance, wishing for gold gets them a heap of old coins no shop will take, and asking to be "beautiful" lands them in hot water when strangers won''t leave them alone.
As the series rolls on, the siblings start figuring things out—about responsibility, the tricky limits of magic, and how a little forethought goes a long way. With the Psammead''s quirks becoming clearer, they muddle through funny scrapes and the occasional close call, growing tighter as a family along the way. By the end, they''ve had enough of the chaos and realize the best joys don''t need magic. So, they say goodbye to the grumpy old Sand Fairy and its unpredictable tricks, happy to settle for the simpler stuff in life.', 'Ended', NULL, '', 5.4, 7, '1991-01-09', '1991-02-13', 1.2082, 'en', 'Five Children and It', '', 6, 1, 0, 'Miniseries', 'GB', '/pbIzLjA0RjerCxuzZf5jev5cpOV.jpg', '/6rTfIKanct0SDxlmbQr9Iq6enYD.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:18:29Z', '2026-04-03T17:18:34Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('ee466bc7-7f7e-403b-8175-057733bf9af3', 'Schoolhouse Rock!', 10077, 78642, 1973, 'Schoolhouse Rock! is an American interstitial programming series of animated musical educational short films that aired during the Saturday morning children''s programming on the U.S. television network ABC. The topics covered included grammar, science, economics, history, mathematics, and civics. The series'' original run lasted from 1973 to 1985, and was later revived with both old and new episodes airing from 1993 to 1999. Additional episodes were produced as recently as 2009 for direct-to-video release.', 'The series is structured as an anthology of animated musical educational shorts rather than a traditional narrative show with a continuing storyline. Each segment operates as a self-contained vignette that uses catchy tunes, upbeat rhythms, and colorful animation to teach elementary-level academic concepts. Originally airing as interstitial segments between regular Saturday morning cartoons, the shorts distill complex subjects into memorable, bite-sized lessons. The content is divided into specific thematic categories or "Rocks," which encompass Multiplication Rock, Grammar Rock, America Rock, Science Rock, Computer Rock, Money Rock, and Earth Rock.

Instead of a recurring central cast, the series relies on a rotating lineup of standalone characters and scenarios tailored to the specific educational topic of each song. A typical short centers on a distinct protagonist—often an anthropomorphic object, an animal, or a child—navigating a stylized world that visually demonstrates the concept being taught. For example, in the widely recognized segment "Conjunction Junction," a train conductor hooks together boxcars labeled with words and phrases to demonstrate how conjunctions link parts of a sentence. In "I''m Just a Bill," an anthropomorphic, melancholy piece of legislation sits on the steps of the United States Capitol and explains the lengthy legislative process to a young boy, expressing his anxieties about whether he will survive the gauntlet of Congress to eventually become a law. Other memorable scenarios feature a superhero who illustrates emotional outcries ("Interjections!"), a figure skater performing figure eights to teach multiplication tables ("Figure Eight"), or a woman exploring expanding American territories ("Elbow Room").

The narratives within the shorts frequently blend playful metaphors, humor, and relatable everyday situations to keep young viewers engaged. Whether exploring the central nervous system through the metaphor of a literal telegraph operator, detailing the preamble of the United States Constitution through a folk-style singalong, or following a boy as he learns about the concept of zero from a caped crusader ("My Hero, Zero"), the plots deliberately avoid rigid classroom instruction. Instead, the characters typically discover the educational concept organically through an adventure, a visual puzzle, or a catchy musical chorus that naturally repeats the core lesson. 

Developed on the premise that children can easily memorize popular music lyrics even when they struggle with rote memorization, the series places a strong emphasis on mnemonic learning. The underlying mission of the shorts is to make fundamental academic subjects—ranging from math and civics to environmentalism and economics—accessible, entertaining, and deeply memorable. By employing creative storytelling and blending pop, rock, folk, and jazz musical styles with imaginative animation, the series provides a foundational educational experience designed to seamlessly integrate learning into weekend entertainment.', 'Ended', 'ABC', '', 7.5, 33, '1973-01-06', '2009-12-17', 3.3725, 'en', 'Schoolhouse Rock!', '', 65, 7, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/4HCCVNl90rdcv1SJXCbktmVHTt4.jpg', '/scpUWDiKk6SCiNOBk0mVU58tV9f.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:13:54Z', '2026-04-03T17:14:28Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('eea5207a-afb0-4a84-9824-b6c77e32e7c5', 'The Real Housewives of Salt Lake City', 110381, 372309, 2020, 'Fueled by multi-million-dollar businesses and brands, a hidden social circle of successful women have created their own paradise in the majestic mountains of Utah.', '*The Real Housewives of Salt Lake City* premiered on November 11, 2020, serving as the first major expansion of the franchise in several years. The first season introduced an ensemble consisting of Lisa Barlow, Mary Cosby, Heather Gay, Meredith Marks, Whitney Rose, and Jen Shah. Early storylines highlighted the women''s navigation of Utah''s unique religious and cultural landscape, prominently featuring Gay and Rose''s highly emotional journeys of leaving the strict Mormon church, juxtaposed with Barlow''s self-proclaimed "Mormon 2.0" lifestyle. The season heavily focused on Cosby''s highly scrutinized marriage to her late grandmother''s second husband, as well as Shah''s extreme emotional volatility, erratic outbursts, and escalating feud with Marks over Marks''s son Brooks.

The second season added Jennie Nguyen and was entirely dominated by one of the most explosive and unprecedented moments in reality television history: Shah''s shocking on-camera arrest by federal agents for her involvement in a massive, nationwide telemarketing fraud scheme. The arrest, which occurred in a filming van moments before the cast departed for a trip to Vail, triggered intense paranoia and vicious infighting, with Shah aggressively accusing Marks of tipping off the FBI. Simultaneously, the season chronicled mounting skepticism surrounding Cosby''s unorthodox church practices, which other cast members likened to a cult. Refusing to face the allegations, Cosby skipped the season two reunion and subsequently departed the series. Shortly after the season aired, Bravo fired Nguyen following the discovery of deeply offensive and racist social media posts.

The third season introduced Angie Harrington, Angie Katsanevas, and Danna Bui-Negrete in "friend of" capacities. The narrative closely followed Shah''s steadfast declarations of innocence regarding her federal charges, leading up to her shocking decision to plead guilty to wire fraud just weeks before her trial. The season also chronicled the spectacular collapse of Barlow and Marks''s longstanding friendship following a leaked, profanity-laced "hot mic" audio recording of Barlow disparaging Marks. The cast dynamics were further fractured during an infamous, chaotic trip to San Diego where Gay mysteriously acquired a severely bruised black eye overnight. Gay''s steadfast, highly controversial refusal to explain the cause of her injury deeply frustrated the group and fundamentally shattered her tight-knit alliance with Rose, known to fans as "Bad Weather." Following the season''s conclusion, Shah was sentenced to 6.5 years in federal prison and officially exited the series.

The fourth season promoted Katsanevas to a full-time housewife, added Monica Garcia to the main cast, and featured Cosby''s return in a "friend of" capacity. The season initially focused on the shifting alliances in Shah''s absence, Katsanevas''s struggles to firmly integrate into the core group, and Garcia''s turbulent relationship with her overbearing mother. However, the season ultimately built to a historic, highly publicized climax during a cast trip to Bermuda. In a dramatic, explosive confrontation, Gay produced a meticulously researched dossier proving that Garcia was the secret mastermind behind "Reality Von Tease," a vicious anonymous Instagram troll account that had aggressively cyberbullied, exposed, and tormented the cast for years. The shocking betrayal resulted in Garcia being universally ostracized by the women and subsequently fired from the network.

The fifth season introduced Bronwyn Newport, while Cosby was promoted back to a full-time housewife alongside new "friends of" Britani Bateman and Meili Workman. Storylines in the post-Garcia era focused heavily on the group attempting to rebuild fractured trust, Newport''s eccentric extreme-wealth lifestyle, and a bitter, season-long feud that severely deteriorated Barlow and Rose''s friendship. The season''s narrative was also heavily driven by Katsanevas''s mounting tensions with both Cosby and Bateman, as well as the deeply complicated, constantly shifting power dynamics between longtime frenemies Barlow and Marks.', 'Returning Series', 'Bravo', '', 6.0, 22, '2020-11-11', '2026-01-27', 10.3381, 'en', 'The Real Housewives of Salt Lake City', 'https://www.bravotv.com/the-real-housewives-of-salt-lake-city', 113, 7, 1, 'Reality', 'US', '/1tQ2wVVKoma672CxE2kfl9oxHR8.jpg', '/52C5LxFmg4z0dSUUbYnLhJij0Jv.jpg', '2026-04-03T16:35:43Z', '2026-04-03T16:57:30Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('f21c45c2-bea9-4347-8d0d-977e1a496ce3', 'Round the Twist', 695, 75677, 1990, 'Round the Twist is a Logie Award-winning Australian children''s television series about three children and their father who live in a lighthouse and become involved in many bizarre magical adventures.', 'Round the Twist is set around an old lighthouse in the fictional Port Niranda, on the rugged southwest Victorian coast and features the Twist family: fourteen-year-old twins Pete and Linda, eight-year-old son Bronson, and father Tony, a widowed artist who makes sculptures. Each episode finds the Twist kids involved in surreal, supernatural adventures.
The series has been categorised as humorous, contemporary fantasy.
There were four series of Round the Twist made during the show''s 11-year run. The first 13 half-hour episodes were part of a three-program package the ACTF sold to the Seven Network in September 1988, which also included Kaboodle and The Greatest Tune on Earth. The first series of Round the Twist was based on the popular novels Unreal!, Quirky Tales, Unbelievable!, Cabbage Patch Fib, and Uncanny! by Jennings, who had three books on the Australian children''s bestseller list at the time.
Jennings was the scriptwriter for the first and second series. Storm was the script editor of the first series, co-writer for the second series, and screenwriter and script editor for the third and fourth series. Ray Boseley and Chris Anastassiades were also the writers for the third series. Louise Fox, Christine Madafferi, and Robert Greenberg were assigned the fourth series. The second (1992), third (2000), and fourth series (2001) shared elements with the first regarding style, characterisations, themes, locations, and genres with the original series. The third and fourth series were not based on Jennings'' books, but served as continuations of the first two series.

^ Australian Children''s Television Foundation, (1988). Care for kids: Television News, The newsletter of the Australian Children''s Television Foundation, Issue No. 26, p. 1-4. ISSN 0813-3727

^ Australian Children''s Television Foundation, (1991). Care for kids: Television News, The newsletter of the Australian Children''s Television Foundation, Issue No. 35, p. 1-4. ISSN 0813-3727', 'Canceled', 'ABC TV', 'Rain, rain, go away, come again another day. Have you ever, ever felt like this?', 7.0, 25, '1990-04-06', '2001-05-02', 6.824, 'en', 'Round the Twist', 'http://www.roundthetwist.com/splash_gribbs/splash_gribbs.htm', 52, 4, 0, 'Scripted', 'AU,DE,FR', '/nxOScaEU2exYLBkqG8LiYL69z5l.jpg', '/qYitUEDAV8et1Y60XIYgel5rP3M.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:40:40Z', '2026-04-03T17:40:46Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('f2f13547-c984-48e2-9c9d-8c3eb9824b65', 'VeggieTales', 5024, 79578, 1993, 'American series of children''s computer-animated episodes featuring anthropomorphic vegetables in stories conveying moral themes based on Christianity. They frequently retell Biblical stories, sometimes anachronistically reframed, and include humorous references to pop culture in many different eras by putting Veggie spins on them.', 'The series is framed as a show hosted by anthropomorphic vegetables living on a kitchen countertop, centering primarily on Bob the Tomato, the pragmatic and sensible straight man, and his best friend Larry the Cucumber, who is goofy, imaginative, and energetic. The core narrative structure is driven by a framing device in which Bob and Larry read a letter from a fictional viewer facing a common childhood dilemma, such as dealing with fear, peer pressure, selfishness, or telling the truth. To address the viewer''s problem, the vegetables present one or more stories that serve as allegories or direct lessons. The series blends Christian values with sketch comedy, conveying its moral lessons through an accessible, lighthearted approach that explicitly references Biblical themes and scripture while maintaining a family-friendly sense of humor.

Bob and Larry are joined by a large ensemble cast of recurring vegetable characters who essentially act as a repertory theatre troupe, taking on various roles within the episode''s stories. Prominent members of the cast include Junior Asparagus, a relatable young boy whose experiences often mirror the audience''s everyday struggles; Archibald Asparagus, a stuffy, British-accented intellectual who favors high culture and frequently plays kings or rigid authority figures; Pa Grape, a wise and grandfatherly figure; and Mr. Lunt, a cynical, gold-tooth-sporting gourd. Other frequent contributors include the gluttonous and eccentric brothers Jimmy and Jerry Gourd, the melodramatic Madame Blueberry, and the imposing but largely well-meaning Mr. Nezzer. When not playing specific characters in the main stories, the cast members interact as themselves behind the scenes and on the countertop, establishing a persistent workplace and interpersonal dynamic. 

The central stories of each episode typically fall into two categories: comedic retellings of classic Bible stories—such as David and Goliath, Jericho, or Daniel in the lions'' den—and elaborate parodies of popular culture, literature, and historical events. These parodies frequently place the vegetables in homages to famous franchises, including *Indiana Jones*, *Star Trek*, *The Lord of the Rings*, and classic superhero media, most notably through Larry''s recurring alter-ego, the plunger-headed superhero LarryBoy. A signature stylistic element of the series is a mid-episode musical intermission titled "Silly Songs with Larry." Introduced by an announcer as "the part of the show where Larry comes out and sings a silly song," these segments feature Larry the Cucumber performing absurd, catchy musical numbers about mundane topics, serving as a purely comedic, nonsensical break from the main moral narrative.

Following the conclusion of the main stories, the narrative routinely returns to the kitchen countertop for a final wrap-up. Bob and Larry reflect on the events of the stories and directly connect them back to the original viewer''s letter, ensuring the educational and moral lesson is clearly articulated. They are frequently assisted by Qwerty, an outdated desktop computer that provides a relevant Bible verse to summarize the episode''s central theme. The episodes consistently end on a comforting, prosocial note, concluding with the hosts delivering the series'' signature sign-off directly to the audience: "Remember kids, God made you special, and He loves you very much."', 'Ended', 'NBC', '', 7.4, 27, '1993-12-23', '2015-03-03', 5.5149, 'en', 'VeggieTales', 'http://www.bigidea.com/', 68, 1, 0, 'Scripted', 'US', '/NCQdGCorybrM7YjaxXIA6CEovi.jpg', '/gHSNBXUoEde5nYC6q4EA3e4y53j.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:39:38Z', '2026-04-03T17:40:08Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('f9b2ad86-1209-4164-a113-4082814f1352', 'The Crocodile Hunter', 1904, 90601, 1997, 'Australian host Steve Irwin and his wife Terri run a wildlife refuge. Their shared passion is educating the world about wildlife, including the much feared crocodile and numerous venomous snakes. Steve''s specialty is the capture and relocation of crocodiles. No animal appears too threatening to Steve, his true respect for animals is the foundation for everything he does.', 'The documentary series follows Australian wildlife expert Steve Irwin and his American wife, Terri, as they operate their wildlife refuge—which later became known as the Australia Zoo—in Queensland and travel the globe to promote conservation. The episodes primarily focus on Steve''s hands-on encounters with dangerous or misunderstood animals, particularly his specialty of tracking, capturing, and relocating formidable predators such as saltwater crocodiles and highly venomous snakes. Known for his high-energy persona, trademark khaki uniform, and enthusiastic catchphrases, Steve utilizes a deeply physical and daring approach to interact with the wildlife. Despite the apparent danger of his methods, his actions are consistently guided by a profound respect for the animals and a desire to keep them safe from human encroachment. 

Terri frequently accompanies him on these expeditions, actively assisting in the captures and providing a grounded, informative counterpoint to Steve''s exuberance. Throughout the series'' run, the couple embarks on adventures across the rugged Australian Outback as well as various international locations, including Africa, Asia, and the Americas. By intimately showcasing the unique behaviors of these creatures in their natural habitats, the Irwins aim to educate the public, debunk pervasive myths about the world''s most feared predators, and advocate for the protection of endangered species.', 'Ended', 'Animal Planet', '', 7.6, 33, '1997-04-05', '2004-06-18', 5.9687, 'en', 'The Crocodile Hunter', '', 69, 5, 0, 'Documentary', 'AU', '/youExJaJ4EW8jjDllu0emOJAIKi.jpg', '/v48dSrKSKD3C5aLJofx4bnhYwQ.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:39:09Z', '2026-04-03T17:39:30Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('fd00c1ef-7ec7-4f90-bbec-f9ebcc6d410d', 'Theodore Tugboat', 3160, 71612, 1993, 'Theodore Tugboat is a Canadian children''s television series about a tugboat named Theodore who lives in the Big Harbour with all of his friends. The show originated in Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada as a co-production between the CBC, and the now defunct Cochran Entertainment, and was filmed on a model set using radio controlled tugboats, ships, and machinery. Production of the show ended in 2001, and its distribution rights were later sold to Classic Media. The show premiered in Canada on CBC Television, then went to PBS, was on Qubo in the US, and at one time, had appeared in eighty different countries.

The show deals with life learning issues portrayed by the tugs or other ships in the harbour. Most often, the tugs have a problem, or get involved in a struggle with each other or another ship, but they always manage to help one another resolve these problems and see them through. Their main focus however, is to always make the Big Harbour the friendliest harbour in the world, and to always do a good job with their work related tasks.', 'The series revolves around the daily lives and operations of a fleet of anthropomorphic tugboats who work in the "Big Harbour." The story is framed and narrated by the Dispatcher, a human harbourmaster (played by Denny Doherty), who oversees the operations of the waterway from his office. At the beginning of each episode, the Dispatcher introduces the central theme or problem, directly addressing the audience before assigning the tugboats their work tasks for the day. He serves as a bridge between the human viewers and the animated maritime world, frequently offering guidance, narrating the boats'' adventures, and summarizing the moral lesson at the episode''s conclusion.

The Big Harbour is home to five main tugboats, each distinct in size, color, and personality. The title character, Theodore, is an earnest, friendly tugboat wearing a red baseball cap who is always eager to learn and help others. He works alongside George, the largest and strongest ocean tug, who is occasionally boastful but deeply protective of his friends; Emily, the sole female tug in the main fleet, who wears an old fisherman''s hat, speaks with a worldly perspective from her travels, and is known for her vigorous work ethic; Foduck, the harbour''s official safety tug, who wears a red firefighter''s helmet, is equipped with a Very Official Harbour Master (V.O.H.M.) radar, and strictly enforces harbour rules; and Hank, the smallest, youngest, and fastest tug, who wears a blue toque and often struggles with impatience, impulsiveness, or feeling left out due to his small size.

Episodes typically center on life-learning issues and social-emotional development, translating common childhood struggles into maritime scenarios. A typical plot involves one of the tugs encountering a problem—such as making a mistake on the job, feeling jealous of another boat''s assignment, letting their ego get in the way of safety, or struggling to communicate with unfamiliar visiting vessels like cargo ships, submarines, or pilot boats. The tugboats occasionally get into disagreements with one another or try to hide their mistakes out of fear of disappointing the Dispatcher. 

Through teamwork, honest communication, and mutual support, the tugs inevitably manage to solve their problems, complete their assignments, and learn valuable life lessons. The series places a strong educational emphasis on pro-social behavior, demonstrating healthy ways to process complex emotions, the importance of apologizing, and the value of asking for help when overwhelmed. Throughout all of their daily tasks and adventures, the underlying mission of Theodore and his fellow fleet members is to work together to make the Big Harbour "the friendliest harbour in the world."', 'Ended', 'CBC Television', '', 7.0, 1, '1993-07-05', '1993-07-07', 1.5052, 'en', 'Theodore Tugboat', '', 52, 2, 0, 'Scripted', 'CA', '/wIs0tQbZG6zSXCUK7AK287DgMFp.jpg', '/evw3VF7DHRSbZOjPUK2OoB7BaiY.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:10:11Z', '2026-04-03T17:10:40Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO tv_shows (id, title, tmdb_id, tvdb_id, year, overview, plot, status, network, tagline, vote_average, vote_count, first_air_date, last_air_date, popularity, original_language, original_name, homepage, number_of_episodes, number_of_seasons, in_production, type, origin_country, poster_key, backdrop_key, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('ff372c47-9c4a-4c32-81fd-e489570667df', 'Pingu', 9262, 75669, 1986, 'Playful penguin Pingu lives with his family in Antarctica, where he often finds himself caught up in mischievous high jinks with his pal Robby.', 'The series is set in an anthropomorphic penguin community in Antarctica and centers around the daily life and adventures of Pingu, a young, playful, and mischievous penguin who lives with his family in an igloo. A defining narrative characteristic of the series is its complete lack of real-world spoken language; instead, all of the characters communicate in an expressive, babbling gibberish known as "Penguinese." The emotions and plot points are conveyed entirely through body language, tone of voice, and visual gags, often highlighted by Pingu''s signature action of shaping his beak into a megaphone to let out a loud honk, popularly transcribed as "Noot noot!", whenever he is excited, frustrated, or demanding attention. The show explores universal themes of childhood through a comedic lens, utilizing its snowy environment to create unique scenarios involving ice skating, tobogganing, and ice fishing.

The primary focus of the series is on Pingu''s interactions with his family and local community. His household consists of his Mother, a caring but firm parent who manages the igloo; his Father, who works as a local postman delivering mail on a motorized snow-scooter; and his toddler sister, Pinga. The sibling dynamic between Pingu and Pinga is a frequent source of plotlines, heavily exploring themes of rivalry, sharing, and eventual cooperation. While Pingu often feels jealous of the attention Pinga receives and finds himself annoyed by her tagging along, he ultimately demonstrates a strong, protective affection for her. Outside of his home, Pingu''s most prominent companion is his best friend Robby, a clever and playful seal. Though their relationship begins with playful antagonism over a fishing dispute in their first meeting, they quickly become inseparable companions who frequently engage in practical jokes and collaborative misadventures. Other recurring characters include Pingu''s penguin friends Pingo and Pingg, his eventual love interest Pingi, and his beloved Grandfather, an elderly penguin who lives in his own igloo and often plays the accordion.

Episodes typically follow a slice-of-life structure driven by Pingu''s curiosity, stubbornness, or penchant for mischief. Storylines routinely begin with mundane childhood situations, such as being asked to do chores, refusing to eat certain meals, playing games with friends, or struggling to behave in a school setting. Pingu''s impulsive actions or attempts to cut corners usually lead to comedic trouble, minor property damage, or conflicts with his peers. When his schemes unravel or his parents intervene to reprimand him, Pingu frequently displays exaggerated temper tantrums, stretching his clay body or crying. However, the narrative always concludes on a reassuring, prosocial note. After facing the natural consequences of his misbehavior, Pingu learns a mild moral lesson, takes responsibility by apologizing or fixing his mistakes, and is warmly forgiven by his family, emphasizing themes of family unity, emotional growth, and the importance of owning up to one''s actions.', 'Ended', 'CBeebies', '', 6.9, 157, '1986-05-28', '2006-03-03', 8.2629, 'en', 'Pingu', '', 156, 6, 0, 'Scripted', 'CH,GB', '/nLE7NR3EsEbv2NfG0bLIaOEhGfY.jpg', '/10UOUxOjmLCwGOvNXNaiIdWXav.jpg', '2026-04-03T17:22:18Z', '2026-04-03T17:22:51Z');

